(window.MAP_DATA = window.MAP_DATA || {})["reading_6"] = {"grade":6,"subject":"reading","units":[{"id":"R4","n":4,"title":"RIT 4","goal":"RIT","band":"181–190","skills":["Determines theme in literary text"],"vocab":[],"sets":[{"p":0,"goal":"LT","items":[{"n":1,"t":"mc","s":"Which statement BEST expresses the theme of the story?","o":["Merchants are difficult customers.","Pottery is an old craft.","Careful, patient work matters more than speed.","Older people always know best, so the young should obey."],"k":"C","sk":"Determines theme in literary text","b":"181–190","g":"LT"}],"set":"1"}]},{"id":"R5","n":5,"title":"RIT 5","goal":"RIT","band":"191–200","skills":["Determines main/central idea in informational text","Understands sequence in informational text","Applies knowledge of Greek or Latin roots in the 6-8 grade band","Uses context and roots to determine word meaning"],"vocab":[],"sets":[{"p":0,"goal":"LT","items":[{"n":1,"t":"mc","s":"“Hamza’s face burned.” What does this phrase show?","o":["Hamza stood too close to the oven.","Hamza felt ashamed.","Hamza was excited.","Hamza had a fever."],"k":"B","sk":"Uses context to determine the meaning of a nonliteral phrase","b":"191–200","g":"LT"},{"n":2,"t":"mc","s":"What is the effect of repeating the line “Clay remembers every hurry” at the end?","o":["It shows that clay can really remember things.","It shows that Hamza has forgotten his master’s lesson completely.","It shows that Master Idris is still alive.","It shows that Hamza has made his master’s lesson his own."],"k":"D","sk":"Interprets the use of repetition in context","b":"191–200","g":"LT"},{"n":3,"t":"tp","s":"Part A: Which word BEST describes Hamza at the beginning of the story?","o":["overconfident","lazy","unkind","frightened"],"k":"A","sk":"Describes character traits; supporting evidence","b":"191–200","g":"LT","pb":{"s":"Which detail BEST supports the answer to Part A?","o":["He wedged the clay longer.","“I will finish them by tonight,” Hamza promised.","Hamza had worked in Master Idris’s pottery shop for a year.","People travelled from other towns to buy Hamza’s pottery."],"k":"B"}}],"set":"1"},{"p":1,"goal":"LT","items":[{"n":1,"t":"mc","s":"Which sentence BEST states a theme of the story?","o":["Little brothers often cause problems for older brothers.","Competitions are unfair to students who work hard.","Real skill grows through patience and careful practice.","Calligraphy is an old art."],"k":"C","sk":"Determines theme in literary text","b":"191–200","g":"LT"}],"set":"2"},{"p":2,"goal":"IT","items":[{"n":1,"t":"mc","s":"What is the central idea of the text?","o":["Desert beetles have bumpy backs that help them collect tiny drops of water from fog.","Chile and Morocco have coasts.","Fog nets are a simple way to collect water in dry places where fog is common.","Strong winds can damage the poles and nets that villages build."],"k":"C","sk":"Determines main/central idea in informational text","b":"191–200","g":"IT"},{"n":2,"t":"mc","s":"What happens immediately AFTER droplets stick to the threads?","o":["They join together and become heavy.","They are cleaned and filtered for drinking.","They turn into fog.","They run down into a pipe and a tank."],"k":"A","sk":"Understands sequence in informational text","b":"191–200","g":"IT"},{"n":3,"t":"mc","s":"Why do scientists study desert beetles?","o":["The beetles drink the water that the nets collect.","The beetles live only in Chile.","The beetles damage the nets by climbing on them.","The beetles collect water from fog in a similar way."],"k":"D","sk":"Analyzes implicit relationships between ideas","b":"191–200","g":"IT"}],"set":"3"},{"p":3,"goal":"IT","items":[{"n":1,"t":"mc","s":"What is the central idea of the text?","o":["Trees give shade.","Animals need grass to eat, so farmers should grow more of it.","Desertification is a serious problem with known causes, and people are working to solve it.","Deserts are hot, dry places where very few plants and animals can live for long without water."],"k":"C","sk":"Determines main/central idea in informational text","b":"191–200","g":"IT"},{"n":2,"t":"mc","s":"According to the text, what happens AFTER plants are removed from the land?","o":["The wind blows the topsoil away.","Animals stop grazing.","More rain falls.","The soil becomes richer and darker."],"k":"A","sk":"Understands sequence in informational text","b":"191–200","g":"IT"},{"n":3,"t":"mc","s":"What does the word **desertification** mean in the text?","o":["the planting of trees and shrubs in the desert","the process of land slowly turning into desert","the study of deserts","a storm in the desert"],"k":"B","sk":"Determines domain-specific word meaning in context","b":"191–200","g":"IT"}],"set":"4"},{"p":4,"goal":"VO","items":[{"n":1,"t":"mc","s":"The root **rupt** means “break.” Which word means “to break into a conversation”?","o":["corrupt","erupt","rupture","interrupt"],"k":"D","sk":"Applies knowledge of Greek or Latin roots in the 6-8 grade band","b":"191–200","g":"VO"},{"n":2,"t":"mc","s":"Read the sentence. “The ancient stone had an **inscription** that told the name of the king who built the wall.” What is an **inscription**?","o":["a map of an ancient city","a hole cut into a wall for light and air","words carved or written on something","a kind of stone"],"k":"C","sk":"Uses context and roots to determine word meaning","b":"191–200","g":"VO"},{"n":3,"t":"mc","s":"Which word BEST completes the sentence? “Angry at the referee’s decision, the coach ___ at him across the field.”","o":["smiled","glared","peeked","glanced"],"k":"B","sk":"Analyzes nuances in meaning among related words","b":"191–200","g":"VO"},{"n":4,"t":"mc","s":"Read the sentence. “The scientist will **present** her findings at the conference.” Which meaning of **present** fits?","o":["to show or explain to an audience","the time that is happening now","to be in a place","a gift"],"k":"A","sk":"Determines which meaning of a multiple-meaning word fits","b":"191–200","g":"VO"}],"set":"5"},{"p":null,"goal":"VO","items":[{"n":1,"t":"mc","s":"The root **rupt** means “break.” When a volcano **erupts**, it —","o":["stays quiet","bursts out suddenly","grows taller","cools down slowly"],"k":"B","sk":"Applies knowledge of Greek or Latin roots in the 6-8 grade band","b":"191–200","g":"VO"},{"n":2,"t":"mc","s":"The root **vis** means “see,” and the prefix **in-** means “not.” What does **invisible** mean?","o":["not able to be seen","very bright","easy to see","seen again and again"],"k":"A","sk":"Uses context and roots to determine word meaning","b":"191–200","g":"VO"}],"set":"6"},{"p":5,"goal":"LT","items":[{"n":1,"t":"mc","s":"What point of view is the story told from?","o":["first person, by the boy who is lost","second person","first person, by the uncle","third person, by an outside narrator"],"k":"A","sk":"Recognizes first-person point of view","b":"191–200","g":"LT"}],"set":"7"},{"p":6,"goal":"LT","items":[{"n":1,"t":"mc","s":"From which point of view is the story told?","o":["second person, speaking directly to the reader","first person, by Sultan","first person, by Sultan’s father, the captain of the fishing boat","third person, mostly following Sultan’s thoughts and feelings"],"k":"D","sk":"Recognizes third-person point of view","b":"191–200","g":"LT"}],"set":"8"},{"p":7,"goal":"IT","items":[{"n":1,"t":"mc","s":"What is the author’s attitude toward school gardens?","o":["not interested","strongly against","strongly in favour","unsure"],"k":"C","sk":"Determines author’s attitude in informational text","b":"191–200","g":"IT"}],"set":"9"},{"p":8,"goal":"IT","items":[{"n":1,"t":"mc","s":"What is the author’s attitude toward swimming lessons?","o":["uninterested","uncertain","mildly against it","strongly in favour"],"k":"D","sk":"Determines author’s attitude in informational text","b":"191–200","g":"IT"}],"set":"10"},{"p":9,"goal":"Literary and Informational Text (paired)","items":[{"n":1,"t":"mc","s":"“Every step feels like lifting a sack of rice.” What does this simile show?","o":["The climber is hungry.","The path is flat.","The climber is carrying rice.","Climbing is extremely tiring."],"k":"D","sk":"Interprets simile in context","b":"191–200","g":"Literary and Informational Text (paired)"},{"n":2,"t":"mc","s":"Why do climbers spend weeks moving between camps, according to Text 2?","o":["to take photographs of the mountain views","to let their bodies acclimatize to the altitude","to find the best places to put their tents on the mountain","to wait for supplies"],"k":"B","sk":"Determines the cause of a situation","b":"191–200","g":"Literary and Informational Text (paired)"}],"set":"11"},{"p":10,"goal":"Literary and Informational Text (paired)","items":[{"n":1,"t":"mc","s":"According to Text 2, why does rain in the desert cause sudden floods?","o":["It rains every day.","The hard, dry ground cannot absorb the water quickly.","Deserts have many hidden rivers flowing under the sand.","The sand soaks up all of the rainwater at once."],"k":"B","sk":"Determines the cause of a situation","b":"191–200","g":"Literary and Informational Text (paired)"}],"set":"12"}]},{"id":"R6","n":6,"title":"RIT 6","goal":"RIT","band":"201–210","skills":["Analyzes the development of theme in literary text","Identifies problem-solution as a structure","Analyzes how specific paragraphs contribute to meaning","Uses paragraph-level context to determine word meaning"],"vocab":[],"sets":[{"p":0,"goal":"LT","items":[{"n":1,"t":"mc","s":"How does the theme develop over the story?","o":["The merchant teaches Hamza how to make jugs.","Master Idris learns from Hamza that working faster is better for business.","Hamza is careful and patient at first, but he becomes careless and proud after his success.","Hamza values speed, fails, learns from his master and finally passes the lesson on."],"k":"D","sk":"Analyzes the development of theme in literary text","b":"201–210","g":"LT"},{"n":2,"t":"mc","s":"Why does Master Idris NOT scold Hamza?","o":["He wants Hamza to understand the lesson for himself.","He thinks the merchant is wrong.","He did not notice the broken jugs on the shelf.","He is afraid that the merchant will stop buying from him."],"k":"A","sk":"Analyzes dialogue to understand characters","b":"201–210","g":"LT"}],"set":"1"},{"p":1,"goal":"LT","items":[{"n":1,"t":"mc","s":"How does the author develop the theme across the story?","o":["by showing Yazeed slowly change from rushing to working patiently","by describing the old bookshop and the streets of the city in great detail","by explaining the history of Thuluth script","by listing the rules of the calligraphy competition"],"k":"A","sk":"Analyzes the development of theme in literary text","b":"201–210","g":"LT"},{"n":2,"t":"mc","s":"What does Master Idris mean when he says, “A letter that runs cannot breathe”?","o":["The paper is too thin.","Letters written too quickly lack space and balance.","Yazeed is writing in the wrong kind of script for this line.","The ink is too wet and runs across the page."],"k":"B","sk":"Uses context to determine the meaning of a nonliteral phrase","b":"201–210","g":"LT"},{"n":3,"t":"mc","s":"What does Yazeed’s reaction to the spilled tea show?","o":["He no longer cares about the competition.","He wants Master Idris to fix the page.","He is angry with his brother.","He has learned to stay calm and patient."],"k":"D","sk":"Analyzes how a character responds to challenges","b":"201–210","g":"LT"},{"n":4,"t":"mc","s":"Which detail BEST supports the idea that Master Idris is a wise teacher?","o":["He lives in a small room above the old bookshop in the market.","He nods slowly when he sees the final page.","He is old.","He asks Yazeed to write only one letter at a time."],"k":"D","sk":"Describes character traits; supporting evidence","b":"201–210","g":"LT"}],"set":"2"},{"p":2,"goal":"IT","items":[{"n":1,"t":"mc","s":"Which structure does the author use for the first TWO paragraphs?","o":["description of a person","problem and solution","compare and contrast","chronological order"],"k":"B","sk":"Identifies problem-solution as a structure","b":"201–210","g":"IT"},{"n":2,"t":"mc","s":"What is the purpose of the fourth paragraph?","o":["to explain the limits of fog nets and where they are used","to tell a story about a village","to argue that fog nets are useless in most places","to describe, step by step, how villagers build and repair a fog net"],"k":"A","sk":"Analyzes how specific paragraphs contribute to meaning","b":"201–210","g":"IT"},{"n":3,"t":"mc","s":"Which sentence is the BEST objective summary of the text?","o":["Fog nets are the best invention in the world, and every village everywhere should build many of them today.","Villages in dry places should move to rainy places instead of building nets.","Engineers in Chile like fog.","Fog nets catch droplets from fog and turn them into usable water, though they work only in foggy places."],"k":"D","sk":"Summarizes informational text","b":"201–210","g":"IT"}],"set":"3"},{"p":3,"goal":"IT","items":[{"n":1,"t":"mc","s":"How is the text mainly organized?","o":["It gives steps for planting a tree.","It compares the deserts of two different countries.","It tells the story of one farm in time order, from the past to today.","It explains a problem, its causes and possible solutions."],"k":"D","sk":"Identifies problem-solution as a structure","b":"201–210","g":"IT"},{"n":2,"t":"mc","s":"How does the second paragraph contribute to the text?","o":["It defines the word desert.","It gives the author’s opinion.","It describes solutions to the problem.","It explains why desertification happens."],"k":"D","sk":"Analyzes how specific paragraphs contribute to meaning","b":"201–210","g":"IT"}],"set":"4"},{"p":4,"goal":"VO","items":[{"n":1,"t":"mc","s":"Read the paragraph. “At first, the old man seemed **aloof**. He sat apart from the others, rarely smiled and answered questions with a single word. Only later did we learn that he was simply shy.” What does **aloof** mean?","o":["kind, warm and friendly","loud and funny","distant and unfriendly","tired and ill"],"k":"C","sk":"Uses paragraph-level context to determine word meaning","b":"201–210","g":"VO"},{"n":2,"t":"mc","s":"“My little brother has the patience of a saint when he builds his models.” What does this phrase suggest?","o":["He is extremely patient.","He is very religious.","He loses his temper easily.","He builds models quickly."],"k":"A","sk":"Interprets allusion and figurative language in context","b":"201–210","g":"VO"}],"set":"5"},{"p":null,"goal":"VO","items":[{"n":1,"t":"mc","s":"Which word suggests the STRONGEST feeling of dislike?","o":["mind","avoid","dislike","detest"],"k":"D","sk":"Analyzes nuances in meaning among related words","b":"201–210","g":"VO"},{"n":2,"t":"mc","s":"Read the paragraph. “The old map was **illegible**. Water had washed away most of the ink, and the few words that remained were faded and smudged. Even with a magnifying glass, we could not read the names of the towns.” What does **illegible** mean?","o":["very large","very old","impossible to read","beautifully drawn"],"k":"C","sk":"Uses paragraph-level context to determine word meaning","b":"201–210","g":"VO"},{"n":3,"t":"mc","s":"Read the sentence. “The scientist will **conduct** an experiment to test her idea.” Which meaning of **conduct** fits?","o":["to lead an orchestra","to carry out or organize","to allow heat or electricity to pass through","a person’s behaviour"],"k":"B","sk":"Determines which meaning of a multiple-meaning word fits","b":"201–210","g":"VO"},{"n":4,"t":"mc","s":"The root **photo** means “light.” In **photosynthesis**, plants use —","o":["soil to make photographs of the garden","heat to make roots","light to make their own food","water to make light"],"k":"C","sk":"Applies knowledge of Greek or Latin roots in the 6-8 grade band","b":"201–210","g":"VO"}],"set":"6"},{"p":5,"goal":"LT","items":[{"n":1,"t":"mc","s":"How does the first-person point of view affect the story?","o":["Readers know what every character is thinking at all times.","Readers share the narrator’s fear and relief directly.","Readers know the ending before it happens.","Readers learn the uncle’s thoughts."],"k":"B","sk":"Analyzes the effect of narrator’s point of view","b":"201–210","g":"LT"},{"n":2,"t":"mc","s":"What mood does the second paragraph create?","o":["cheerful and calm","angry and loud","funny and silly","tense and uneasy"],"k":"D","sk":"Determines mood in context","b":"201–210","g":"LT"},{"n":3,"t":"mc","s":"“The dunes around me rose like frozen waves.” What does this imagery help the reader understand?","o":["The weather was cold and snowy.","The dunes were moving quickly in the wind.","The dunes were huge, endless and all alike.","The narrator was near the sea."],"k":"C","sk":"Analyzes the effect of imagery in context","b":"201–210","g":"LT"},{"n":4,"t":"mc","s":"How does the setting affect the narrator?","o":["The camp is too noisy for him to sleep.","The strong wind blows him back toward the camp.","The hot sun makes him so tired that he falls asleep on the sand until morning.","The silent, dark desert frightens him but also forces him to think carefully."],"k":"D","sk":"Analyzes how setting affects characters","b":"201–210","g":"LT"},{"n":5,"t":"mc","s":"If the uncle told this story, what would MOST LIKELY be different?","o":["Readers would see the worry at the camp instead of the boy’s fear in the dunes.","There would be no campfire.","The boy would never be found, and the story would end sadly.","The story would take place in a busy city instead of the desert, at a different time."],"k":"A","sk":"Predicts how a story would differ if told from another point of view","b":"201–210","g":"LT"}],"set":"7"},{"p":6,"goal":"LT","items":[{"n":1,"t":"mc","s":"How does the point of view affect the story?","o":["Readers hear the story from the boat itself.","Readers experience the storm through Sultan’s fear and his trust in his father.","Readers learn the long history of fishing in the Gulf.","Readers know everything the father is thinking and planning as he steers the boat."],"k":"B","sk":"Analyzes the effect of narrator’s point of view","b":"201–210","g":"LT"},{"n":2,"t":"mc","s":"How does the mood change from the first paragraph to the third?","o":["from sad to happy","from frightening and tense to calm and peaceful","from calm and peaceful to frightening and tense","from funny and light to serious and sad"],"k":"C","sk":"Determines mood in context","b":"201–210","g":"LT"},{"n":3,"t":"mc","s":"How does the setting affect Sultan?","o":["The violent storm frightens him, but it also teaches him to trust his father.","The harbour makes him want to stay on land forever.","The rain makes him fall asleep.","The calm, sunny sea makes him feel bored, sleepy and eager to go home to the harbour."],"k":"A","sk":"Analyzes how setting affects characters","b":"201–210","g":"LT"},{"n":4,"t":"mc","s":"What does the last paragraph reveal about Sultan?","o":["He has forgotten the storm.","Although the storm frightened him, it made him love the sea even more.","He never wants to go fishing with his father again after such a storm.","He is angry with his father for taking him to sea."],"k":"B","sk":"Analyzes how a character changes","b":"201–210","g":"LT"}],"set":"8"},{"p":7,"goal":"IT","items":[{"n":1,"t":"mc","s":"What is the author’s claim?","o":["Science tests in the city’s schools are too difficult.","Every school in the city should have a student-run vegetable garden.","Students in the city should eat more salad and vegetables at lunch.","Drip irrigation saves water."],"k":"B","sk":"Determines author’s main argument or claim","b":"201–210","g":"IT"},{"n":2,"t":"mc","s":"Which evidence BEST supports the idea that gardens improve learning?","o":["Students in a garden program scored higher on science tests.","Gardens need water.","Students in the program chose more salad at lunch.","Companies in the city donate seeds and tools to schools every spring."],"k":"A","sk":"Evaluates evidence used to support claims","b":"201–210","g":"IT"},{"n":3,"t":"mc","s":"What is the purpose of the fourth paragraph?","o":["to give a new reason for gardens","to describe a garden","to tell an anecdote","to answer a counterclaim"],"k":"D","sk":"Identifies a counterclaim and rebuttal","b":"201–210","g":"IT"},{"n":4,"t":"mc","s":"The author calls a garden “a classroom without walls.” Why does the author use this metaphor?","o":["to show that classrooms are too small for students","to describe the size of the garden","to explain that school gardens do not need fences or walls around them","to persuade readers that gardens are real places of learning"],"k":"D","sk":"Analyzes how word choice advances purpose","b":"201–210","g":"IT"}],"set":"9"},{"p":8,"goal":"IT","items":[{"n":1,"t":"mc","s":"What is the author’s claim?","o":["Swimming pools are too expensive for schools.","Children should exercise every day.","Every school should teach all students to swim.","Drowning is common at the beach."],"k":"C","sk":"Determines author’s main argument or claim","b":"201–210","g":"IT"},{"n":2,"t":"mc","s":"Which evidence BEST supports the safety reason?","o":["the World Health Organization’s information about drowning","the claim about better marks","the idea that swimming is gentle on the joints and strengthens the heart","the cost of building pools"],"k":"A","sk":"Evaluates evidence used to support claims","b":"201–210","g":"IT"}],"set":"10"},{"p":9,"goal":"Literary and Informational Text (paired)","items":[{"n":1,"t":"mc","s":"How is Text 1 different from Text 2?","o":["Text 1 shares one climber’s feelings; Text 2 presents facts about the mountain.","Text 1 is about a journey by sea; Text 2 is about a mountain.","Both texts are diaries.","Text 1 gives statistics about the mountain; Text 2 tells one climber’s personal story."],"k":"A","sk":"Compares and contrasts multiple texts","b":"201–210","g":"Literary and Informational Text (paired)"},{"n":2,"t":"mc","s":"Which fact from Text 2 BEST explains why the climber in Text 1 counts his breaths?","o":["Raha Moharrak reached the summit in 2013.","Climbers move between camps for several weeks before the summit.","The air at the summit has only about one-third as much oxygen as at sea level.","Everest is 8,849 metres high, which makes it the highest mountain on Earth."],"k":"C","sk":"Synthesizes information found in multiple texts","b":"201–210","g":"Literary and Informational Text (paired)"},{"n":3,"t":"mc","s":"Why does the climber decide to try for the summit even though he is frightened?","o":["He has extra oxygen that the others do not have.","His team forces him.","He is thinking of his father’s unfinished climb.","The weather is perfect, and there is no wind."],"k":"C","sk":"Understands how challenges influence characters’ thoughts or actions","b":"201–210","g":"Literary and Informational Text (paired)"}],"set":"11"},{"p":10,"goal":"Literary and Informational Text (paired)","items":[{"n":1,"t":"mc","s":"How are the two texts different?","o":["Both texts are articles that give statistics and safety advice about wadis.","Text 2 describes the uncle’s car.","Text 1 gives scientific facts about floods in wadis; Text 2 tells one family’s personal story of a flood.","Text 1 shows one family’s experience of a flood; Text 2 explains flash floods and gives safety advice."],"k":"D","sk":"Compares and contrasts multiple texts","b":"201–210","g":"Literary and Informational Text (paired)"},{"n":2,"t":"mc","s":"“Brown water roared through it … as fast as a river in a hurry.” What does this simile emphasize?","o":["how deep the wadi was","the bright colour of the desert sand","the speed and power of the flood","how calm the water was"],"k":"C","sk":"Interprets simile in context","b":"201–210","g":"Literary and Informational Text (paired)"},{"n":3,"t":"mc","s":"Why does the uncle turn the car around?","o":["He understands that crossing the flooded wadi would be too dangerous.","He wants to drive around the wadi to help the driver of the truck.","He has forgotten the way home in the dark.","The rain has stopped."],"k":"A","sk":"Understands how challenges influence characters’ thoughts or actions","b":"201–210","g":"Literary and Informational Text (paired)"}],"set":"12"}]},{"id":"R7","n":7,"title":"RIT 7","goal":"RIT","band":"211–220","skills":["Determines a shared theme in multiple texts","Interprets the use of repetition in context","Writes or identifies an objective summary","Analyzes implicit relationships between ideas"],"vocab":[],"sets":[{"p":1,"goal":"LT","items":[{"n":1,"t":"mc","s":"Why does the author repeat the word “breathe” at the end of the story?","o":["to show that Master Idris is ill and needs to rest","to show that Yazeed has finally learned Master Idris’s lesson","to describe the smell of the ink","to show that Yazeed is very tired after working all night on his page"],"k":"B","sk":"Interprets the use of repetition in context","b":"211–220","g":"LT"},{"n":2,"t":"mc","s":"Which is the BEST objective summary of the story?","o":["Yazeed rushes his calligraphy until his teacher makes him practise slowly; when his page is ruined, he patiently starts again and earns his teacher’s praise.","Yazeed is a gifted calligrapher who deserved first prize for his beautiful page, but the judges unfairly gave the prize to someone else and he came second.","Yazeed’s careless little brother ruins his page, so Yazeed must work all night to finish in time.","Master Idris is a strict teacher who wastes Yazeed’s time."],"k":"A","sk":"Writes or identifies an objective summary","b":"211–220","g":"LT"}],"set":"1"},{"p":3,"goal":"IT","items":[{"n":1,"t":"mc","s":"Why would planting native shrubs be MORE useful than planting plants that need a lot of water?","o":["Native shrubs grow much taller than other plants.","Native shrubs can survive with little water in dry areas.","Native shrubs do not have roots.","Plants that need a lot of water grow faster in sand and dry soil."],"k":"B","sk":"Analyzes implicit relationships between ideas","b":"211–220","g":"IT"},{"n":2,"t":"mc","s":"Which is the BEST summary of the text?","o":["Overgrazing, cutting trees and drought turn dry land into desert, but planting, controlled grazing and careful farming can help, and Saudi Arabia plans to plant ten billion trees.","Animals eat too much grass, so they should be removed from all farms.","Desertification is a terrible problem, and everyone should feel sad and worried about the future of the land.","Saudi Arabia plans to plant ten billion trees within its borders in the coming decades, restore damaged land and protect its deserts, so the problem will soon be solved."],"k":"A","sk":"Summarizes informational text","b":"211–220","g":"IT"}],"set":"2"},{"p":null,"goal":"VO","items":[{"n":1,"t":"mc","s":"“Cleaning the whole stadium in one hour was a **Herculean** task.” The word **Herculean** is an allusion to Hercules, a hero famous for great strength. What does it suggest about the task?","o":["It was dangerous for animals.","It was quick and easy for one person.","It was done by one hero.","It needed enormous strength and effort."],"k":"D","sk":"Interprets allusion and figurative language in context","b":"211–220","g":"VO"}],"set":"3"},{"p":6,"goal":"LT","items":[{"n":1,"t":"mc","s":"“A bruise-coloured line of cloud.” What does this image suggest?","o":["The clouds are dark and threatening, like an injury.","Someone has been hurt.","The clouds are bright and colourful, like a sunset.","The clouds are far away and will not cause harm."],"k":"A","sk":"Analyzes the effect of imagery in context","b":"211–220","g":"LT"},{"n":2,"t":"mc","s":"If the story were told by Sultan’s father, the reader would MOST LIKELY learn more about —","o":["what Sultan dreamed about later that night when he was in his bed","the history of the boat and how its engine works","what the father noticed and planned as he steered through the storm","the fish in the net"],"k":"C","sk":"Predicts how a story would differ if told from another point of view","b":"211–220","g":"LT"}],"set":"4"},{"p":7,"goal":"IT","items":[{"n":1,"t":"mc","s":"Which statement about the evidence is MOST accurate?","o":["The author gives no numbers at all.","The Riyadh example is a single case, so it does not prove that every school would get the same result.","All of the evidence comes from the author’s own opinions and feelings, not from any study or survey of students.","The study proves that school gardens cost nothing to build or run."],"k":"B","sk":"Analyzes inadequately supported claims","b":"211–220","g":"IT"}],"set":"5"},{"p":8,"goal":"IT","items":[{"n":1,"t":"mc","s":"How does the author respond to the counterclaim in the fourth paragraph?","o":["by changing the claim","by agreeing it is a fair concern and then suggesting partnerships with public pools","by saying that pools are cheap and easy to build","by ignoring the counterclaim completely and repeating the first reason again more loudly"],"k":"B","sk":"Identifies a counterclaim and rebuttal","b":"211–220","g":"IT"},{"n":2,"t":"mc","s":"Why does the author use the phrase “a child’s life” in the fourth paragraph?","o":["to describe what happens in a swimming lesson","to explain exactly how much money it costs to build a new pool in a school","to give a statistic","to make the cost of lessons seem small compared with what is at stake"],"k":"D","sk":"Analyzes how word choice advances purpose","b":"211–220","g":"IT"},{"n":3,"t":"mc","s":"The phrase “everyone knows” in the last paragraph is an example of —","o":["a claim that sounds certain but gives no proof","a scientific fact that is supported by research","a counterclaim","a quotation from an expert on swimming"],"k":"A","sk":"Recognizes persuasive techniques","b":"211–220","g":"IT"}],"set":"6"},{"p":9,"goal":"Literary and Informational Text (paired)","items":[{"n":1,"t":"mc","s":"Which theme is shared by both texts?","o":["Reaching a great goal requires courage and perseverance.","Oxygen is expensive.","Mountains are dangerous places for people to live.","Diaries are more interesting to read than articles about climbing."],"k":"A","sk":"Determines a shared theme in multiple texts","b":"211–220","g":"Literary and Informational Text (paired)"}],"set":"7"},{"p":10,"goal":"Literary and Informational Text (paired)","items":[{"n":1,"t":"mc","s":"Which information from Text 2 helps explain what happened to the pickup truck in Text 1?","o":["Wadis are valleys.","Moving water only about 30 centimetres deep can carry away a small car.","In desert regions, most of the year passes without any rain.","Safety experts say that people should never try to walk across a flooded wadi."],"k":"B","sk":"Synthesizes information found in multiple texts","b":"211–220","g":"Literary and Informational Text (paired)"},{"n":2,"t":"mc","s":"What does the uncle mean by “The desert keeps its rivers hidden … until it decides to show them”?","o":["Rivers appear only at night.","There are secret rivers flowing under the sand.","Dry wadis can suddenly fill with dangerous water.","The desert is alive."],"k":"C","sk":"Determines the meaning of a nonliteral phrase","b":"211–220","g":"Literary and Informational Text (paired)"},{"n":3,"t":"mc","s":"Which idea is shared by BOTH texts?","o":["Floods happen only in cities.","Deserts almost never get any rain at all.","Cars can easily cross wadis if they drive fast enough through them.","Flash floods are powerful and must be treated with respect."],"k":"D","sk":"Determines a shared theme in multiple texts","b":"211–220","g":"Literary and Informational Text (paired)"}],"set":"8"}]},{"id":"R8","n":8,"title":"RIT 8","goal":"RIT","band":"221–230","skills":["Analyzes inadequately supported claims"],"vocab":[],"sets":[{"p":8,"goal":"IT","items":[{"n":1,"t":"mc","s":"Which claim in the text is NOT supported by evidence?","o":["Swimming exercises the whole body.","Schools can partner with public pools.","Children who swim get better marks in every subject.","Drowning is a leading cause of injury death for children."],"k":"C","sk":"Analyzes inadequately supported claims","b":"221–230","g":"IT"}],"set":"1"}]}],"mocks":[{"id":"A","note":"Full practice test · 43 questions","sections":[{"passage":{"title":"The Second Chance","genre":"story","paras":["Rayan had always been the fastest runner in his class, so when he twisted his ankle a week before the city race, he felt as if the world had stopped. The doctor said he could not run for a month.","For the first few days, Rayan refused to go to practice. He sat in his room and stared at his running shoes. Then his coach called. “The team needs someone to time the runners and keep records,” he said. “Will you do it?”","At first Rayan agreed only because his mother insisted. But as he stood at the side of the track with a stopwatch, he began to notice things he had never seen while running: how Faisal started too quickly and tired at the end, how Omar’s arms swung across his body. He wrote everything down and shared his notes with the coach.","On race day, Faisal paced himself carefully and finished second, his best result ever. He ran to Rayan and hugged him. “Your notes helped me,” he said. Rayan looked at his ankle and laughed. He had not run a single step, yet he felt that he had been part of the race."]},"items":[{"n":1,"t":"mc","s":"How does Rayan feel when he first hurts his ankle?","o":["proud","excited and full of hope","deeply disappointed","relieved"],"k":"C","sk":"Infers character feelings or thoughts","b":"181–190","g":"LT"},{"n":2,"t":"mc","s":"What causes Rayan’s attitude to change?","o":["His mother buys him new shoes for the next race.","He discovers he can help the team by observing and taking notes.","His ankle heals much faster than expected, and he can run again.","He wins the city race."],"k":"B","sk":"Understands how characters are developed or changed","b":"191–200","g":"LT"},{"n":3,"t":"mc","s":"What is the theme of the story?","o":["There are many ways to contribute to a team.","Injuries always ruin plans.","Coaches should not call students at home.","Running is the best sport."],"k":"A","sk":"Determines theme in literary text","b":"191–200","g":"LT"},{"n":4,"t":"mc","s":"Which sentence BEST shows that Rayan has become valuable to the team?","o":["The doctor said he could not run for a month.","He sat in his room and stared at his running shoes.","Rayan had always been the fastest runner in his class.","“Your notes helped me,” he said."],"k":"D","sk":"Determines details that support an inference","b":"201–210","g":"LT"},{"n":5,"t":"mc","s":"Which word BEST describes Rayan as he keeps records for the team?","o":["observant","jealous","lazy","careless"],"k":"A","sk":"Describes character traits; supporting evidence","b":"201–210","g":"LT"}]},{"passage":{"title":"The House of Wisdom","genre":"informational","paras":["More than a thousand years ago, Baghdad was home to one of the greatest centres of learning in the world: the House of Wisdom (Bayt al-Hikma). It was supported by the Abbasid caliphs, especially al-Ma’mun, who ruled from 813 to 833.","Scholars at the House of Wisdom translated books on mathematics, medicine, astronomy and philosophy from Greek, Persian and Indian languages into Arabic. Translation was not just copying. The scholars corrected errors, compared different sources and added new ideas of their own.","One of the most famous scholars linked to this period was al-Khwarizmi. His book on calculation gave its name to **algebra**, and the word **algorithm** comes from his name.","Because these works were preserved in Arabic, they were later translated into Latin and studied across Europe. In this way, the House of Wisdom helped carry knowledge from the ancient world to the modern one."]},"items":[{"n":6,"t":"mc","s":"Which words come from al-Khwarizmi’s work and name?","o":["astronomy and medicine","Baghdad and Abbasid","Latin and Greek","algebra and algorithm"],"k":"D","sk":"Locates details in informational text","b":"181–190","g":"IT"},{"n":7,"t":"mc","s":"What is the central idea of the text?","o":["Al-Ma’mun ruled from 813 to 833 and supported many scholars and translators in Baghdad.","Baghdad is an old city that was once the capital of a great empire.","The House of Wisdom preserved and developed knowledge that later spread to the world.","Algebra is a difficult subject."],"k":"C","sk":"Determines main/central idea","b":"191–200","g":"IT"},{"n":8,"t":"mc","s":"According to the text, why was translation “not just copying”?","o":["Books were translated only from Arabic into Greek.","Scholars corrected errors, compared sources and added new ideas.","Translators did not read the books.","Scholars wrote very slowly and carefully by hand, so it took years."],"k":"B","sk":"Determines details that support a stated idea","b":"201–210","g":"IT"},{"n":9,"t":"mc","s":"What is the effect described in the last paragraph?","o":["Europe stopped using Latin and began using Arabic.","The House of Wisdom was destroyed and its books were lost in a fire.","Knowledge preserved in Arabic was later studied across Europe.","Scholars moved to India."],"k":"C","sk":"Determines the effect of a situation or event","b":"201–210","g":"IT"},{"n":10,"t":"mc","s":"According to the text, who supported the House of Wisdom?","o":["al-Khwarizmi alone","scholars and kings from Europe and Persia","merchants from India and Persia","the Abbasid caliphs, especially al-Ma’mun"],"k":"D","sk":"Locates details in informational text","b":"201–210","g":"IT"}]},{"passage":{"title":"Desert Night","genre":"poem","paras":["The sun lies down behind the hill,","And all the burning sand grows still.","The sky pulls on its velvet gown","With silver buttons stitched in town.","","The fox steps out on silent feet,","Escaping from the day’s long heat.","And I, who feared the dark when small,","Now think it is the best of all."],"poem":true},"items":[{"n":11,"t":"mc","s":"“The sky pulls on its velvet gown.” What literary device is used here?","o":["personification","hyperbole","onomatopoeia","rhyme only"],"k":"A","sk":"Interprets personification in context","b":"191–200","g":"LT"},{"n":12,"t":"mc","s":"What are the “silver buttons” in the first stanza?","o":["the moon’s shadows","stars","grains of sand","raindrops"],"k":"B","sk":"Interprets metaphor in context","b":"201–210","g":"LT"},{"n":13,"t":"mc","s":"How has the speaker’s feeling about the dark changed?","o":["He was afraid of it as a child but now loves it.","He loved it as a child but now he fears it a lot.","He has always feared it.","He has never noticed it."],"k":"A","sk":"Determines speaker’s attitude in poetry","b":"201–210","g":"LT"},{"n":14,"t":"mc","s":"What is the mood of the poem?","o":["angry and wild","busy and noisy","calm and peaceful","sad and lonely"],"k":"C","sk":"Analyzes how mood is conveyed in context","b":"201–210","g":"LT"},{"n":15,"t":"mc","s":"How does the focus of the poem shift from the first stanza to the second?","o":["from a storm to a calm day","from the night sky to a desert animal and the speaker’s own feelings","from the busy city at night to the calm sea at dawn","from the speaker’s own feelings to a description of the morning sun"],"k":"B","sk":"Analyzes how specific parts of a poem contribute to meaning","b":"211–220","g":"LT"}]},{"passage":{"title":"Should Homework Be Banned?","genre":"argument","paras":["**Viewpoint 1 — Mona, teacher:** Homework gives students extra practice. Skills such as reading and mathematics improve when students practise a little every day. Homework also teaches responsibility and helps parents see what their children are learning.","**Viewpoint 2 — Saud, Grade 6 student:** After seven hours at school, many students are tired. Long homework leaves little time for sports, family or sleep. I think short reading at home would be better than worksheets, because it is enjoyable and still builds skills."]},"items":[{"n":16,"t":"mc","s":"What does Saud suggest instead of worksheets?","o":["longer school days","more mathematics","no learning at home","short reading at home"],"k":"D","sk":"Locates details in informational text","b":"191–200","g":"IT"},{"n":17,"t":"mc","s":"Which reason does Saud give for his view?","o":["Homework teaches students responsibility and good habits.","Teachers need more time.","Homework helps parents see what their children are learning.","Long homework leaves little time for sports, family or sleep."],"k":"D","sk":"Identifies reasons that support a claim","b":"201–210","g":"IT"},{"n":18,"t":"mc","s":"On what point do Mona and Saud AGREE?","o":["Students should do more worksheets.","Practice at home can build skills.","Sports are more important than school.","Homework should be banned."],"k":"B","sk":"Compares and contrasts viewpoints in multiple texts","b":"211–220","g":"IT"},{"n":19,"t":"mc","s":"How is Mona’s viewpoint mainly supported?","o":["with a chart of test scores from her classes","with a personal story about her own son’s homework at night","with reasons about practice, responsibility and parents","with a quotation from a scientist"],"k":"C","sk":"Evaluates evidence used to support claims","b":"211–220","g":"IT"},{"n":20,"t":"mc","s":"How is Saud’s viewpoint DIFFERENT from Mona’s?","o":["Saud thinks long homework takes away needed rest and activities; Mona thinks homework builds skills and responsibility.","Saud wants more worksheets and less reading at home; Mona wants fewer worksheets and much more reading every evening at home.","Saud thinks reading is boring and useless; Mona thinks reading is the most important subject.","There is no difference between them."],"k":"A","sk":"Compares and contrasts viewpoints in multiple texts","b":"211–220","g":"IT"}]},{"passage":{"title":"The Robot That Would Not Turn","genre":"story","paras":["For three months, Ziad and his team built a small robot for the regional robotics competition. It could follow a black line across a board, pick up a block and place it in a box. In their school laboratory, it worked perfectly every time.","At the competition, the robot rolled forward smoothly, reached the first corner and kept going straight, right off the edge of the board. The audience groaned. On the second try, the same thing happened. Ziad’s face burned. He wanted to pick up the robot and walk out of the hall.","Instead, his teammate Anas knelt beside the board and stared at it. “The lights,” he said slowly. “The hall lights are much brighter than the lab. Our sensor can’t see the black line at the corner.”","They had ten minutes before their final attempt. Ziad took a deep breath. Together they changed the sensor setting, tested it on a sheet of paper under the bright lights and put the robot back on the board. This time it turned at the corner, picked up the block and dropped it neatly into the box.","They finished fifth, not first. On the bus home, Ziad opened his notebook and wrote at the top of a new page: *Things the lab did not teach us.* Under it he wrote the first item: *Test where you will compete.*"]},"items":[{"n":21,"t":"mc","s":"“Ziad’s face burned.” What does this show about Ziad?","o":["The hall was very hot.","He was excited.","He felt embarrassed and upset.","He had a fever and felt very ill."],"k":"C","sk":"Determines details that reveal characters’ feelings","b":"201–210","g":"LT"},{"n":22,"t":"mc","s":"How does Anas respond to the problem?","o":["He blames Ziad for building the robot badly.","He stays calm and looks carefully for the cause.","He walks out of the hall.","He asks the judges for a new board and a new start."],"k":"B","sk":"Analyzes how a character responds to challenges","b":"201–210","g":"LT"},{"n":23,"t":"mc","s":"Why did the robot work in the lab but fail at the competition?","o":["The brighter lights in the hall stopped the sensor from seeing the line.","Someone changed the program.","The robot’s battery was almost empty at the competition.","The competition board was smaller and narrower than the board in the lab."],"k":"A","sk":"Determines the cause of an event in literary text","b":"211–220","g":"LT"},{"n":24,"t":"mc","s":"What does Ziad’s new notebook page suggest about him?","o":["He is angry with the judges.","He wants to quit robotics.","He believes the team should have won the first prize.","He has decided to learn from the failure."],"k":"D","sk":"Analyzes the development of theme in literary text","b":"211–220","g":"LT"},{"n":25,"t":"mc","s":"Which sentence BEST states the theme?","o":["Failure can teach lessons that success never does.","Bright lights are dangerous.","Only first place matters in a competition.","Robots are difficult to build and often break in competitions."],"k":"A","sk":"Determines theme in literary text","b":"211–220","g":"LT"}]},{"passage":{"title":"Old Jeddah: A City Built for the Sea Breeze","genre":"informational","paras":["In the centre of modern Jeddah lies its old district, known as **Al-Balad**. Its narrow streets and tall, pale houses date back hundreds of years, to a time when Jeddah was the main port for pilgrims travelling to Makkah. In 2014, Historic Jeddah was added to UNESCO’s World Heritage List.","**Building with the Sea**","Many houses in Al-Balad were built from blocks of coral stone cut from the reefs of the Red Sea. Coral stone is light and full of tiny holes, so it does not hold heat as strongly as heavy rock. Builders joined the blocks with mud and strengthened the walls with wooden beams.","**Windows That Breathe**","The most famous features of the houses are the **rawashin**: large wooden bay windows covered with carved screens. The screens let the sea breeze flow through the rooms while keeping out the strong sunlight. They also allowed families to look out at the street without being seen.","**Saving the Past**","Over time, many families moved to newer parts of the city, and some old houses fell into ruin. Today, restoration projects are repairing the buildings so that future generations can see how people once lived in harmony with a hot climate."]},"items":[{"n":26,"t":"mc","s":"What is the central idea of the text?","o":["Jeddah is a large modern city on the coast of the Red Sea with many new buildings, roads and bridges.","Pilgrims travel to Makkah.","Old Jeddah’s buildings were cleverly designed for a hot, coastal climate and are now being preserved.","Coral grows in the reefs of the Red Sea and can be cut into blocks."],"k":"C","sk":"Determines main/central idea in informational text","b":"211–220","g":"IT"},{"n":27,"t":"mc","s":"Why was coral stone a good building material in Jeddah?","o":["It is found far from the sea.","It is stronger and heavier than steel or concrete.","It is very colourful and makes the houses beautiful to look at.","It is light and does not hold heat as strongly as heavy rock."],"k":"D","sk":"Determines the cause of a situation","b":"211–220","g":"IT"},{"n":28,"t":"mc","s":"Which TWO purposes did the rawashin serve? Choose the answer that includes BOTH.","o":["storing drinking water and keeping food cool for the family","letting in the breeze while blocking strong sunlight, and giving families privacy","keeping out the noise of the busy street and lighting the rooms at night with lamps","decorating the street and holding signs"],"k":"B","sk":"Determines details that support a concept","b":"211–220","g":"IT"},{"n":29,"t":"mc","s":"What is the purpose of the section “Saving the Past”?","o":["to compare Jeddah with other cities","to explain how the old houses declined and are now being restored","to describe how blocks of coral stone are cut and joined","to tell the history of the pilgrims who passed through Jeddah to Makkah"],"k":"B","sk":"Determines specific purpose of a subsection","b":"211–220","g":"IT"},{"n":30,"t":"mc","s":"What does the phrase “in harmony with a hot climate” suggest about the old builders?","o":["They built houses only in winter.","They did not care about the heat when they built houses.","They played music in the houses to help people forget about the heat.","They designed buildings that worked with the heat instead of against it."],"k":"D","sk":"Analyzes how word choice advances purpose","b":"221–230","g":"IT"}]},{"passage":null,"items":[{"n":31,"t":"mc","s":"“The exam was a breeze.” What does this mean?","o":["The exam was very easy.","The exam was cold.","The exam was outside.","The exam was very long."],"k":"A","sk":"Interprets metaphor in context","b":"181–190","g":"VO"},{"n":32,"t":"mc","s":"The root **chron** means “time.” Which word describes events listed in the order they happened?","o":["photographic","thermal","chronological","visible"],"k":"C","sk":"Applies knowledge of Greek or Latin roots","b":"191–200","g":"VO"},{"n":33,"t":"mc","s":"Which word has the most POSITIVE connotation?","o":["stubborn","pushy","determined","headstrong"],"k":"C","sk":"Understands precise connotations of words","b":"191–200","g":"VO"},{"n":34,"t":"mc","s":"Read the sentence. “The company had to **reject** the plan because it cost too much.” Which word is an ANTONYM of **reject**?","o":["refuse","accept","deny","decline"],"k":"B","sk":"Identifies synonyms or antonyms in the 6-8 grade band","b":"191–200","g":"VO"},{"n":35,"t":"mc","s":"Read the sentence. “Please **file** your reports in the blue folder.” Which meaning of **file** fits?","o":["a line of people","a computer program","a tool for smoothing your nails","to put papers in order in a place"],"k":"D","sk":"Determines which meaning of a multiple-meaning word fits","b":"191–200","g":"VO"},{"n":36,"t":"mc","s":"Read the sentence. “The documents were **confidential**, so only the manager was allowed to read them.” What does **confidential** mean?","o":["private or secret","expensive","very old","badly written and messy"],"k":"A","sk":"Uses context to determine the meaning of words in the 6-8 grade band","b":"201–210","g":"VO"},{"n":37,"t":"mc","s":"Read the sentence. “The speaker’s words were **concise**; she said everything important in under a minute.” What does **concise** mean?","o":["brief and clear","long and confusing","funny and silly","loud and angry"],"k":"A","sk":"Uses context to confirm word meaning","b":"201–210","g":"VO"},{"n":38,"t":"mc","s":"“That new bicycle **cost an arm and a leg**.” What does this mean?","o":["It was given away free.","It was very cheap.","It caused an injury.","It was very expensive."],"k":"D","sk":"Interprets idiom in context","b":"201–210","g":"VO"},{"n":39,"t":"mc","s":"In which sentence does **draft** mean “an early version of a piece of writing”?","o":["Football clubs draft new players each year.","The cold draft from the window made us shiver.","I wrote a first draft of my essay last night.","Sit away from the draft near the door."],"k":"C","sk":"Determines which meaning of a multiple-meaning word fits","b":"201–210","g":"VO"},{"n":40,"t":"mc","s":"Which word BEST completes the sentence? “The road was closed; ___, we had to take a longer route.”","o":["for instance","consequently","similarly","however"],"k":"B","sk":"Identifies words that show a cause-effect relationship","b":"211–220","g":"VO"},{"n":41,"t":"mc","s":"The root **spect** means “look.” What does **in retrospect** mean?","o":["looking very closely at something small","looking back at the past","looking forward to the future","not looking at all"],"k":"B","sk":"Applies knowledge of Greek or Latin roots in the 6-8 grade band","b":"211–220","g":"VO"},{"n":42,"t":"mc","s":"Which word has the most NEGATIVE connotation?","o":["careful","economical","thrifty","stingy"],"k":"D","sk":"Understands precise connotations of words","b":"211–220","g":"VO"},{"n":43,"t":"mc","s":"Read the sentence. “The instructions were **ambiguous**; half the class understood them one way, and half another.” What does **ambiguous** mean?","o":["very short","perfectly clear","having more than one possible meaning","written in another language that nobody knows"],"k":"C","sk":"Uses context to determine word meaning","b":"211–220","g":"VO"}]}]},{"id":"B","note":"Full practice test · 43 questions","sections":[{"passage":{"title":"Hegra: A City Carved in Stone","genre":"informational","paras":["In the desert near AlUla in northwest Saudi Arabia stand more than a hundred huge tombs carved directly into sandstone mountains. This ancient site is called **Hegra**, and in 2008 it became the first place in Saudi Arabia to be listed as a UNESCO World Heritage Site.","**Who Built Hegra?**","Hegra was built by the **Nabataeans**, a people who also built the famous city of Petra in what is now Jordan. About two thousand years ago, they controlled important trade routes. Caravans carrying incense, spices and other valuable goods passed through their cities, making the Nabataeans wealthy.","**Art in the Rock**","Rather than building with bricks, Nabataean workers cut the tombs from the top of the cliff downward. Many tombs have decorated doorways, carved eagles and writing above the entrance that tells who the tomb was made for.","**Hegra Today**","For many years, few visitors came to Hegra. Today, it is open to tourists, and scientists continue to study the site to learn more about how the Nabataeans lived."]},"items":[{"n":1,"t":"mc","s":"According to the section “Art in the Rock,” how were the tombs made?","o":["Workers cut them from the top of the cliff downward.","Workers built them with bricks.","Workers dug them underground.","Workers moved large stones from far away by camel."],"k":"A","sk":"Locates details in informational text","b":"191–200","g":"IT"},{"n":2,"t":"mc","s":"What is the purpose of the subheadings?","o":["to list the sources","to give the author’s opinion about each tomb","to show the dates of each tomb","to organize the information into topics"],"k":"D","sk":"Analyzes the purpose of text features","b":"191–200","g":"IT"},{"n":3,"t":"mc","s":"What is the central idea of the text?","o":["AlUla is a town in the northwest of Saudi Arabia that has many visitors every year from many countries.","Hegra is an ancient Nabataean site of carved tombs that is important to history and heritage.","Tourists like the desert.","Incense and spices were valuable goods carried by caravans long ago."],"k":"B","sk":"Determines main/central idea in informational text","b":"201–210","g":"IT"},{"n":4,"t":"mc","s":"Why were the Nabataeans wealthy?","o":["They grew many crops.","They found large amounts of gold and silver in the sandstone mountains.","They controlled trade routes used by caravans carrying valuable goods.","They sold the carved tombs to rich travellers."],"k":"C","sk":"Determines the cause of a situation","b":"201–210","g":"IT"},{"n":5,"t":"mc","s":"What does the detail about Petra add to the text?","o":["It shows that the Nabataeans were skilled builders of more than one great site.","It shows that Petra is also found in Saudi Arabia.","It explains exactly where AlUla is located today and how to travel there by car.","It describes the eagles on the tombs."],"k":"A","sk":"Analyzes how specific details contribute to meaning","b":"211–220","g":"IT"}]},{"passage":{"title":"The Interview","genre":"story","paras":["For his history project, Basim had to interview someone about life fifty years ago. He chose his great-grandfather, mostly because the old man lived next door and the project was due the next day.","Basim set his phone to record and asked his first question quickly: “What was school like?” His great-grandfather laughed. “School? School was a mat under a tree and a teacher with a stick for pointing at the board. We learned our letters by writing them in the sand.”","Basim had planned to ask five questions and leave. But one story led to another: walking to the well before sunrise, the first time the family saw an electric light, the year the road reached the village. Basim stopped looking at the clock. He began asking questions that were not on his list.","When he finally stopped the recording, two hours had passed. His great-grandfather looked tired but pleased. “Nobody has asked me these things for a long time,” he said.","That night, Basim did not just write his project. He started a notebook titled “Grandfather’s Stories,” and he wrote on the first page: *To be continued.*"]},"items":[{"n":6,"t":"mc","s":"Why does Basim first choose his great-grandfather for the interview?","o":["His great-grandfather asks him to record his stories.","It is convenient because he lives next door and the project is due soon.","He loves history more than any other subject at school and wants to be a historian.","His teacher tells him to."],"k":"B","sk":"Explains character motivation","b":"191–200","g":"LT"},{"n":7,"t":"mc","s":"“Basim stopped looking at the clock.” What does this detail show?","o":["Basim’s phone has stopped recording the interview.","It is very late.","Basim is in a hurry to leave and finish his project.","Basim has become truly interested in the stories."],"k":"D","sk":"Determines details that support an inference","b":"201–210","g":"LT"},{"n":8,"t":"mc","s":"What does the great-grandfather’s comment, “Nobody has asked me these things for a long time,” suggest?","o":["He does not want to talk about the past at all, because it makes him sad.","He wants to be paid for his stories.","He has felt that his memories were forgotten or unimportant to others.","He has a poor memory and forgets most things."],"k":"C","sk":"Analyzes dialogue to understand characters","b":"201–210","g":"LT"},{"n":9,"t":"mc","s":"Which theme is developed in the story?","o":["Phones are useful tools for recording interviews.","Listening to older people can connect us to the past and to each other.","Homework should be done early.","School was easier in the past than it is today, so students had more free time."],"k":"B","sk":"Analyzes the development of theme in literary text","b":"201–210","g":"LT"},{"n":10,"t":"mc","s":"What is the significance of the words *To be continued* at the end?","o":["Basim plans to keep listening to and recording the stories.","Basim wants to write a novel.","The project is not finished on time.","The great-grandfather will tell a story tomorrow only and then stop."],"k":"A","sk":"Analyzes how an ending contributes to meaning","b":"211–220","g":"LT"}]},{"passage":{"title":"Ink","genre":"poem","paras":["A drop of ink is small and still,","A sleeping seed upon the sill.","But touch it with a patient pen,","And watch it wake and walk again.","","It climbs the page in curving lines,","It builds its cities, draws its signs.","It carries voices, far and old,","Of stories that were never told.","","So if you think your words are few,","Remember what one drop can do."],"poem":true},"items":[{"n":11,"t":"mc","s":"In the first stanza, the poet compares a drop of ink to —","o":["a small city","a curving line","a sleeping seed","a patient pen"],"k":"C","sk":"Interprets metaphor in context","b":"191–200","g":"LT"},{"n":12,"t":"mc","s":"“And watch it wake and walk again.” Which kind of figurative language is used?","o":["hyperbole","simile","onomatopoeia","personification"],"k":"D","sk":"Interprets personification in context","b":"191–200","g":"LT"},{"n":13,"t":"mc","s":"Who is the speaker addressing in the last two lines?","o":["the reader","the ink","the pen","a teacher"],"k":"A","sk":"Analyzes narrative elements in poetry","b":"201–210","g":"LT"},{"n":14,"t":"mc","s":"What does the second stanza suggest that writing can do?","o":["stop people from speaking with their own voices and their own ideas","only record numbers and lists for the future","damage paper","create worlds and carry ideas and voices across time"],"k":"D","sk":"Analyzes how specific parts of a poem contribute to meaning","b":"211–220","g":"LT"},{"n":15,"t":"mc","s":"What is the theme of the poem?","o":["Ink is expensive, so writers should use only a little of it on each page.","Pens should be used slowly and with great patience.","Small beginnings, like a single word, can grow into something powerful.","Cities are built with ink."],"k":"C","sk":"Determines theme in poetry","b":"211–220","g":"LT"}]},{"passage":{"title":"The Weaver’s Knot","genre":"folk tale","paras":["In a mountain village lived a weaver whose carpets were famous across the land. Merchants travelled for weeks to buy them, and kings hung them in their palaces.","A young apprentice watched the weaver work for many months. One day, he noticed something strange. In every carpet, somewhere near the border, the weaver tied one knot in the wrong colour.","“Master,” said the apprentice, “you are the finest weaver alive. Why do you spoil every carpet with a mistake?”","The weaver smiled and continued working. “It is not a mistake,” he said. “It is a reminder. A man who believes his work is perfect stops learning. That one knot keeps my eyes open and my pride small.”","Years later, when the apprentice became a master himself, he too tied one wrong knot in every carpet. And whenever a young weaver asked him why, he told the same story."]},"items":[{"n":16,"t":"mc","s":"What does the apprentice notice about the weaver’s carpets?","o":["Each one has the weaver’s name sewn on it in gold.","Each one has a single knot in the wrong colour.","Each one has a hole in the middle.","Each one is a different size."],"k":"B","sk":"Locates details about plot","b":"191–200","g":"LT"},{"n":17,"t":"mc","s":"Why does the apprentice think the knot is a mistake?","o":["He dislikes the colour the weaver chose for the knot.","The merchants have complained about the carpets.","The king has returned a carpet.","He believes a master weaver should make perfect carpets."],"k":"D","sk":"Makes inferences from literary text","b":"201–210","g":"LT"},{"n":18,"t":"mc","s":"Which sentence BEST states the theme?","o":["Apprentices should not ask their masters questions about their work.","Believing that you are perfect can stop you from growing.","Carpets are valuable.","Kings like beautiful carpets in their palaces."],"k":"B","sk":"Determines theme in literary text","b":"201–210","g":"LT"},{"n":19,"t":"mc","s":"What does the wrong knot symbolize for the weaver?","o":["humility and the need to keep learning","poor eyesight","anger toward the merchants who buy carpets","laziness and a wish to finish quickly"],"k":"A","sk":"Interprets symbolism in literary text","b":"211–220","g":"LT"},{"n":20,"t":"mc","s":"How does the last paragraph contribute to the tale?","o":["It shows that the apprentice failed to learn the lesson.","It describes the village.","It shows that the lesson is passed on from one generation to the next.","It explains how the carpets are sold to kings and merchants in faraway cities."],"k":"C","sk":"Analyzes how specific paragraphs contribute to meaning","b":"211–220","g":"LT"}]},{"passage":{"title":"Reading Survey: Grade 6","genre":"chart","paras":["**Question: How many hours do you read for pleasure each week? (40 students)**","Less than 1 hour: 12 students · 1 to 3 hours: 18 students · 3 to 5 hours: 7 students · More than 5 hours: 3 students","The school library plans to open during lunch break next term to encourage more reading."]},"items":[{"n":21,"t":"mc","s":"Which group has the MOST students?","o":["less than 1 hour","1 to 3 hours","3 to 5 hours","more than 5 hours"],"k":"B","sk":"Interprets information in charts or graphs","b":"191–200","g":"IT"},{"n":22,"t":"mc","s":"How many students read for 3 hours or more each week?","o":["3","7","10","25"],"k":"C","sk":"Interprets information in charts or graphs","b":"201–210","g":"IT"},{"n":23,"t":"mc","s":"Why does the library plan to open during lunch break?","o":["to repair the shelves","to give students more homework at lunch","to sell books","to encourage more students to read"],"k":"D","sk":"Integrates information from graphics and text","b":"201–210","g":"IT"},{"n":24,"t":"mc","s":"What percentage of the students read for pleasure for less than 1 hour a week?","o":["12%","25%","30%","40%"],"k":"C","sk":"Analyzes quantitative information in informational text","b":"211–220","g":"IT"},{"n":25,"t":"mc","s":"Which claim is BEST supported by the survey?","o":["Most of the students read for pleasure for less than 3 hours a week.","Most of the students read for pleasure for more than 5 hours a week.","All students dislike reading.","Students read more at home than they do during the school day."],"k":"A","sk":"Evaluates evidence used to support claims","b":"211–220","g":"IT"}]},{"passage":{"title":"How Vaccines Train the Body","genre":"informational","paras":["Your body is defended by an army of cells called the **immune system**. When harmful germs, such as certain viruses or bacteria, enter the body, the immune system learns to recognize them and makes special proteins called **antibodies** to fight them.","The problem is that the first time the body meets a new germ, it can take days to build a strong defence. During that time, a person may become very ill.","A **vaccine** solves this problem by giving the immune system a safe preview. Vaccines contain weakened or inactive germs, a small harmless part of a germ, or instructions that let the body make that harmless part. The body cannot get the disease from them, but the immune system still learns to recognize the germ and makes antibodies.","After vaccination, the immune system keeps “memory cells.” If the real germ ever enters the body, these cells recognize it quickly and help destroy it before it can cause serious illness. In this way, vaccines have helped reduce many dangerous diseases around the world."]},"items":[{"n":26,"t":"mc","s":"What problem do vaccines help solve?","o":["Germs cannot enter the body.","The body can take days to build a defence against a new germ.","Memory cells forget germs after a few days.","The body makes far too many antibodies when it is healthy."],"k":"B","sk":"Identifies problem-solution as a structure","b":"201–210","g":"IT"},{"n":27,"t":"mc","s":"Why does the author describe the immune system as “an army”?","o":["to help readers understand that it defends the body","to show that the immune system is dangerous to the body","to describe a war between two countries","to explain that it is made of soldiers"],"k":"A","sk":"Analyzes how word choice advances purpose","b":"201–210","g":"IT"},{"n":28,"t":"mc","s":"What do memory cells do?","o":["They stop the body from making any more antibodies.","They create new germs.","They make the person sick.","They recognize the real germ quickly if it returns."],"k":"D","sk":"Locates details in informational text","b":"201–210","g":"IT"},{"n":29,"t":"mc","s":"Why can’t a person get the disease from a vaccine?","o":["It does not contain germs that are able to cause the disease.","It destroys all memory cells.","It contains no ingredients at all, only water.","It is given only to people who are already very ill with the disease."],"k":"A","sk":"Understands explicit relationships between ideas","b":"211–220","g":"IT"},{"n":30,"t":"mc","s":"Which is the BEST summary of the text?","o":["The immune system is an army of cells that makes proteins called antibodies.","Vaccines safely teach the immune system to recognize germs so that it can fight them quickly later.","Germs are everywhere around us, and they can be very dangerous to people of all ages in every country.","Antibodies are proteins."],"k":"B","sk":"Summarizes informational text","b":"211–220","g":"IT"}]},{"passage":null,"items":[{"n":31,"t":"mc","s":"“That bag is as light as a feather.” What does this simile mean?","o":["The bag is full of feathers.","The bag is colourful.","The bag can fly.","The bag is very light."],"k":"D","sk":"Interprets simile in context","b":"191–200","g":"VO"},{"n":32,"t":"mc","s":"The root **scrib** means “write.” What does a **scribe** do?","o":["sells books","paints walls","writes or copies documents","builds houses and walls from stone"],"k":"C","sk":"Applies knowledge of Greek or Latin roots in the 6-8 grade band","b":"201–210","g":"VO"},{"n":33,"t":"mc","s":"Which word suggests the MOST extreme degree of tiredness?","o":["sleepy","exhausted","tired","weary"],"k":"B","sk":"Analyzes nuances in meaning among related words","b":"201–210","g":"VO"},{"n":34,"t":"mc","s":"In which sentence does **bank** mean “the land beside a river”?","o":["We sat on the bank and watched the boats go by.","My father keeps his savings in the bank near our house.","You can bank on me to help you.","The plane began to bank to the left."],"k":"A","sk":"Determines which meaning of a multiple-meaning word fits","b":"201–210","g":"VO"},{"n":35,"t":"mc","s":"“During the final penalty, the goalkeeper showed **nerves of steel**.” What does this mean?","o":["He was very nervous.","He seemed to be made of metal.","He was injured during the match and left the field.","He stayed calm under great pressure."],"k":"D","sk":"Interprets figurative language in context","b":"201–210","g":"VO"},{"n":36,"t":"mc","s":"Read the sentence. “The two countries signed a treaty to end the **conflict**.” What does **conflict** mean?","o":["a journey","a celebration of a national holiday","a serious disagreement or fight","a friendship between two countries"],"k":"C","sk":"Uses context to determine word meaning","b":"201–210","g":"VO"},{"n":37,"t":"mc","s":"The suffix **-ology** means “the study of.” What is **zoology**?","o":["the study of plants only","the study of rocks","the study of animals","the study of stars"],"k":"C","sk":"Uses a defined root and/or affix to determine word meaning","b":"201–210","g":"VO"},{"n":38,"t":"mc","s":"Read the paragraph. “Fahad was usually **gregarious**. At every party, he moved from group to group, telling jokes and meeting new people. That is why it was strange to see him sitting alone.” What does **gregarious** mean?","o":["serious and hard-working","shy and quiet around people","tired","enjoying the company of others"],"k":"D","sk":"Uses paragraph-level context to determine word meaning","b":"211–220","g":"VO"},{"n":39,"t":"mc","s":"Read the sentence. “The small error had a **profound** effect on the results.” What does **profound** mean?","o":["funny","very great","very small","hidden"],"k":"B","sk":"Determines the meaning of academic words in context","b":"211–220","g":"VO"},{"n":40,"t":"mc","s":"Which word BEST completes the sentence? “The first plan failed; ___, the team tried a different approach.”","o":["subsequently","in contrast","for example","similarly"],"k":"A","sk":"Words that show a time or sequence relationship","b":"211–220","g":"VO"},{"n":41,"t":"mc","s":"The root **vid** means “see.” Which word means “proof that can be seen or shown”?","o":["divide","evidence","provide","avoid"],"k":"B","sk":"Applies knowledge of Greek or Latin roots in the 6-8 grade band","b":"211–220","g":"VO"},{"n":42,"t":"mc","s":"Which word means the OPPOSITE of **abundant**?","o":["plentiful","common","large","scarce"],"k":"D","sk":"Identifies antonyms","b":"211–220","g":"VO"},{"n":43,"t":"mc","s":"The word **bittersweet** joins two opposite ideas. **Bittersweet** memories are memories that are —","o":["both happy and sad","only sad","forgotten long ago","only happy"],"k":"A","sk":"Interprets oxymoron in context","b":"211–220","g":"VO"}]}]},{"id":"C","note":"Full practice test · 43 questions · harder","sections":[{"passage":{"title":"The Translator","genre":"story","paras":["When Grandmother arrived from her village to live with us in the city, she spoke only the old dialect of the mountains. My younger brothers could not understand her, so they slowly stopped trying. At dinner she sat quietly while the conversation flowed around her like a river around a rock.","I understood a little of her dialect from summers in the village, but I was embarrassed by my mistakes, so I rarely spoke to her either.","One evening, she was struggling to explain something to the doctor on the telephone. I took the phone and, slowly, made my way through her words. The doctor understood, and Grandmother squeezed my hand so tightly that it hurt.","After that, I became her translator at the pharmacy, at the bank, and finally at our own dinner table. Little by little, my brothers learned her words, and she learned ours. The river, it seemed, had found a way to include the rock."]},"items":[{"n":1,"t":"mc","s":"Why does the narrator rarely speak to Grandmother at first?","o":["She does not want to talk.","He is embarrassed by his mistakes in her dialect.","He does not like his grandmother very much.","He is too busy with homework to talk to her every day."],"k":"B","sk":"Explains character motivation","b":"191–200","g":"LT"},{"n":2,"t":"mc","s":"“The conversation flowed around her like a river around a rock.” What does this simile suggest?","o":["Grandmother was left out of the conversation.","The conversation was about rocks.","Grandmother was talking the most at the dinner table.","The family ate near a river."],"k":"A","sk":"Analyzes the effect of figurative language in context","b":"201–210","g":"LT"},{"n":3,"t":"mc","s":"What is the turning point of the story?","o":["Grandmother arrives from her village to live in the city.","The family eats dinner.","The narrator helps Grandmother speak to the doctor.","The brothers stop trying to talk to her."],"k":"C","sk":"Analyzes climax in literary text","b":"201–210","g":"LT"},{"n":4,"t":"mc","s":"Why does Grandmother squeeze the narrator’s hand “so tightly that it hurt”?","o":["She wants the narrator to hang up.","She is angry with the narrator for taking the phone away from her.","She is afraid of the doctor and what he might say.","She is deeply grateful and relieved that someone understood her."],"k":"D","sk":"Determines details that reveal characters’ feelings","b":"201–210","g":"LT"},{"n":5,"t":"mc","s":"What does the last sentence suggest?","o":["The family has found a way to include Grandmother.","The brothers have stopped speaking.","The river has flooded the house.","Grandmother has decided to return to her village in the mountains."],"k":"A","sk":"Interprets extended metaphor in context","b":"211–220","g":"LT"}]},{"passage":{"title":"The Messenger","genre":"drama","paras":["*(A small shop in an old market. KARIM, a young messenger, rushes in, out of breath.)*","KARIM: Master Yaqub! The ship from Basra has arrived early. The merchants are buying all the silk!","YAQUB: *(calmly closing his account book)* Early, you say? Then the silk will be expensive by noon.","KARIM: But if we do not hurry, we will get nothing!","YAQUB: Karim, a hungry man runs to the first tree, but a wise man waits for the fruit to fall. Tomorrow, when the others have spent their money, the prices will drop.","KARIM: *(slowly sitting down, thinking)* So waiting is also a kind of buying."]},"items":[{"n":6,"t":"mc","s":"How are Karim and Yaqub DIFFERENT?","o":["Karim is a rich merchant, while Yaqub is a young sailor.","Karim is old, while Yaqub is young.","Karim wants to wait, while Yaqub wants to hurry.","Karim is impatient, while Yaqub is calm and thoughtful."],"k":"D","sk":"Compares or contrasts characters","b":"201–210","g":"LT"},{"n":7,"t":"mc","s":"What does Yaqub mean by “a wise man waits for the fruit to fall”?","o":["It is better to eat fruit than to buy silk.","Wise men never buy anything.","It is smarter to wait for the right moment.","Fruit trees grow in the market."],"k":"C","sk":"Determines the meaning of a nonliteral phrase","b":"201–210","g":"LT"},{"n":8,"t":"mc","s":"What does Karim’s last line show?","o":["He is confused about money.","He has understood and accepted Yaqub’s lesson.","He is angry with Yaqub for not hurrying.","He will run to the ship as soon as Yaqub leaves."],"k":"B","sk":"Analyzes how dialogue advances plot","b":"201–210","g":"LT"},{"n":9,"t":"mc","s":"Why does Yaqub believe the prices will drop tomorrow?","o":["Karim will buy all of the silk before noon.","Another ship will sink.","The silk will be damaged by the sea air before tomorrow.","The other merchants will have spent their money by then."],"k":"D","sk":"Makes inferences from literary text","b":"201–210","g":"LT"},{"n":10,"t":"mc","s":"What do the words in italics, such as *(calmly closing his account book)*, tell the reader?","o":["the title of the play and the name of the writer","what the characters say aloud to each other during the scene","how the characters act and where the scene takes place","what happens tomorrow"],"k":"C","sk":"Analyzes use of stage directions","b":"211–220","g":"LT"}]},{"passage":{"title":"The Heat Island Effect","genre":"informational","paras":["On a summer afternoon, the centre of a large city can be several degrees hotter than the countryside around it. Scientists call this the **urban heat island effect**.","The main cause is the materials cities are built from. Dark asphalt and concrete absorb sunlight during the day and release the heat slowly at night. Cars, air conditioners and factories add even more heat. Meanwhile, cities have fewer trees and plants, which normally cool the air by releasing water through their leaves.","The effect is more than uncomfortable. Hotter cities use more electricity for cooling, and extreme heat can be dangerous for older people and young children.","Cities are fighting back. Riyadh’s Green Riyadh project plans to plant millions of trees across the city. Other cities paint roofs white to reflect sunlight or build parks and fountains. Each solution aims to replace heat-trapping surfaces with cooler ones."]},"items":[{"n":11,"t":"mc","s":"What is the main cause of the urban heat island effect, according to the text?","o":["Trees produce heat.","City materials such as asphalt and concrete absorb and release heat.","There is much more rain in cities than in the countryside.","Cities are much closer to the sun than the countryside around them is."],"k":"B","sk":"Determines the cause of a situation or event","b":"201–210","g":"IT"},{"n":12,"t":"mc","s":"Why do painted white roofs help?","o":["They reflect sunlight instead of absorbing it.","They make buildings taller and give more shade.","They release water into the air like plants.","They stop cars from driving."],"k":"A","sk":"Makes inferences from informational text","b":"201–210","g":"IT"},{"n":13,"t":"mc","s":"What is the author’s purpose in the third paragraph?","o":["to persuade people to buy more air conditioners","to show that the heat island effect causes real problems","to explain how trees grow","to describe the history of Riyadh and its parks and gardens"],"k":"B","sk":"Determines specific purpose of a subsection","b":"201–210","g":"IT"},{"n":14,"t":"mc","s":"According to the text, how do trees and plants help cool a city?","o":["They block the wind.","They absorb heat during the day and release it at night in the city.","They reflect sunlight, just like white roofs do.","They release water through their leaves, which cools the air."],"k":"D","sk":"Understands explicit relationships between ideas","b":"201–210","g":"IT"},{"n":15,"t":"mc","s":"How is the text mainly organized?","o":["chronological order, from past to present","a comparison of two cities, Riyadh and another","cause and effect, followed by solutions","a story with characters"],"k":"C","sk":"Describes the organizational structure of a text","b":"211–220","g":"IT"}]},{"passage":{"title":"Make Every Drop Count","genre":"public service announcement","paras":["**Make Every Drop Count!**","A dripping tap can waste more than 20 litres of water a day. That is enough to fill a bucket — twice!","Fix leaks quickly. Turn off the tap while brushing your teeth. Water plants early in the morning, when less water evaporates.","Water is life. In a land where rain is rare, saving water is not a choice. It is our duty to the next generation."]},"items":[{"n":16,"t":"mc","s":"Why should plants be watered early in the morning?","o":["Less water evaporates then.","Plants are thirstier at night.","The water is warmer.","Taps work better in the morning."],"k":"A","sk":"Locates details; cause","b":"191–200","g":"IT"},{"n":17,"t":"mc","s":"What is the main message of the announcement?","o":["People should save water in their daily lives.","Rain is common.","Buckets are useful for collecting rainwater.","Taps are expensive and difficult to repair."],"k":"A","sk":"Determines main idea of public service announcements","b":"201–210","g":"IT"},{"n":18,"t":"mc","s":"Why does the announcement compare the wasted water to filling a bucket twice?","o":["to persuade readers to buy more buckets","to help readers picture how much water is wasted","to describe the weather","to explain how a tap works and why it drips all the time"],"k":"B","sk":"Analyzes techniques used to emphasize ideas","b":"211–220","g":"IT"},{"n":19,"t":"mc","s":"“It is our duty to the next generation.” What persuasive technique does this sentence use?","o":["a statistic about how much water is wasted","a quotation from a water expert","a joke","an appeal to responsibility and the future"],"k":"D","sk":"Analyzes persuasive technique","b":"211–220","g":"IT"},{"n":20,"t":"mc","s":"Based on the announcement, about how much water could ONE dripping tap waste in a week?","o":["more than 1,400 litres","about 7 litres","more than 140 litres","about 20 litres"],"k":"C","sk":"Analyzes quantitative information in informational text","b":"211–220","g":"IT"}]},{"passage":{"title":"The Clockmaker’s Shop","genre":"poem","paras":["A hundred clocks along the wall","Are ticking, ticking, one and all;","Some hurry on with nervous feet,","Some slowly march along the street.","","The clockmaker, with silver hair,","Moves softly round from chair to chair.","He winds them all and sets them right,","And works beneath a single light.","","He told me once, “Each clock’s the same:","It keeps the hours, but not their name.","The clock can count the time you spend;","But you decide what time will mend.”"],"poem":true},"items":[{"n":21,"t":"mc","s":"“Some hurry on with nervous feet.” What does this personification describe?","o":["clocks that have stopped","the clockmaker walking","people running in the street","clocks that tick quickly"],"k":"D","sk":"Interprets personification in context","b":"201–210","g":"LT"},{"n":22,"t":"mc","s":"What effect does the repetition of “ticking, ticking” create?","o":["It describes the clockmaker’s voice.","It imitates the steady sound of the clocks.","It shows that some of the clocks in the shop are broken.","It shows that the shop is silent."],"k":"B","sk":"Interprets the use of repetition in context","b":"201–210","g":"LT"},{"n":23,"t":"mc","s":"What does the second stanza show about the clockmaker?","o":["He is in a hurry to finish and go home.","He is careless and often breaks the clocks.","He is calm and careful with his work.","He dislikes clocks."],"k":"C","sk":"Describes character traits; supporting evidence","b":"201–210","g":"LT"},{"n":24,"t":"mc","s":"What does the clockmaker mean when he says a clock “keeps the hours, but not their name”?","o":["A clock measures time but cannot say what the time is used for.","Clocks in the shop have no numbers written on their faces at all.","Clocks sometimes forget the correct time.","Every hour has a name."],"k":"A","sk":"Determines the meaning of a nonliteral phrase","b":"221–230","g":"LT"},{"n":25,"t":"mc","s":"What is the theme of the poem?","o":["Old shops are dark.","Old clocks are expensive and hard to repair.","We are responsible for how we use our time.","Time moves too slowly when we are waiting."],"k":"C","sk":"Determines theme in poetry","b":"221–230","g":"LT"}]},{"passage":{"title":"The Dunes That Sing","genre":"informational","paras":["Travellers in deserts have long told stories of sand that sings. Some believed the low, booming sound came from spirits hidden in the dunes. Today, scientists know that the sound is real, but its cause is natural.","These **booming dunes** are found in only a few dozen places in the world. The sound usually begins when sand slides down the steep side of a dune, either because of the wind or because a person pushes it. The noise can be a deep hum, like a distant aeroplane, and can sometimes be heard far away.","Not every dune can sing. Scientists have found that booming sand is usually very dry, and its grains tend to be round, smooth and similar in size. When millions of these grains slide together, they seem to move in step, and the dry surface of the dune may act like the skin of a drum, making the sound louder.","Researchers still disagree about some details of how the sound is produced. Each new study, however, brings them closer to explaining one of the desert’s strangest mysteries."]},"items":[{"n":26,"t":"mc","s":"What is the central idea of the text?","o":["Some dunes make booming sounds, and scientists are explaining the natural causes.","Aeroplanes fly over deserts.","Spirits live in the desert dunes and sing whenever travellers pass by on their camels.","All sand dunes sing when the wind blows across them."],"k":"A","sk":"Determines main/central idea in informational text","b":"211–220","g":"IT"},{"n":27,"t":"mc","s":"How does the first paragraph introduce the topic?","o":["It gives steps for making sand sing.","It describes the life of one scientist who studied dunes.","It lists all of the places where dunes are known to sing.","It contrasts old beliefs with what scientists know today."],"k":"D","sk":"Analyzes how specific paragraphs contribute to meaning","b":"211–220","g":"IT"},{"n":28,"t":"mc","s":"Which features does booming sand USUALLY have?","o":["wet grains of many different sizes and colours","dry, round, smooth grains of similar size","sand mixed with mud","sharp, rough grains mixed with mud"],"k":"B","sk":"Locates details in informational text","b":"211–220","g":"IT"},{"n":29,"t":"mc","s":"Why does the author compare the dune’s surface to “the skin of a drum”?","o":["to describe the colour of the sand","to show that people use the dunes as drums at festivals and weddings","to explain how drums are made from animal skin","to help readers understand how the sound might become louder"],"k":"D","sk":"Analyzes how word choice advances purpose","b":"221–230","g":"IT"},{"n":30,"t":"mc","s":"What does the last paragraph suggest about scientific knowledge?","o":["It grows step by step, and some questions are not yet fully answered.","Old stories about singing sand are always correct.","Science cannot explain natural events.","Scientists already know everything about how and why dunes make sound."],"k":"A","sk":"Makes inferences from informational text","b":"221–230","g":"IT"}]},{"passage":null,"items":[{"n":31,"t":"mc","s":"The root **therm** means “heat.” What does a **thermostat** control?","o":["time","sound","temperature","sound volume"],"k":"C","sk":"Applies knowledge of Greek or Latin roots","b":"191–200","g":"VO"},{"n":32,"t":"mc","s":"“The old car was a dinosaur.” What does this metaphor suggest?","o":["The car was very large and green like a dinosaur.","The car was very old and out of date.","The car was in a museum.","The car was dangerous."],"k":"B","sk":"Interprets metaphor in context","b":"191–200","g":"VO"},{"n":33,"t":"mc","s":"Which word BEST completes the sentence? “The two scientists disagreed at first; ___, after more tests, they reached the same conclusion.”","o":["eventually","similarly","instead","unfortunately"],"k":"A","sk":"Uses context to determine the meaning of a phrase","b":"201–210","g":"VO"},{"n":34,"t":"mc","s":"Which word has the most NEGATIVE connotation?","o":["self-assured","arrogant","confident","proud"],"k":"B","sk":"Analyzes connotative meanings of words","b":"201–210","g":"VO"},{"n":35,"t":"mc","s":"“Deafening silence filled the room after the announcement.” What is “deafening silence”?","o":["a loud noise in the room that hurt everyone’s ears badly","a simile that compares silence to music","an oxymoron that shows a very strong, uncomfortable silence","a fact about hearing"],"k":"C","sk":"Interprets oxymoron in context","b":"201–210","g":"VO"},{"n":36,"t":"mc","s":"Read the sentence. “The judge asked the witness to **elaborate** on his answer because it was too short.” What does **elaborate** mean here?","o":["to tell a lie","to whisper","to stop talking and sit down","to give more details"],"k":"D","sk":"Uses context to determine the meaning of words in the 6-8 grade band","b":"201–210","g":"VO"},{"n":37,"t":"mc","s":"A **cyclist** is a person who rides a bicycle. A **pianist** is a person who —","o":["listens to pianos","plays the piano","builds pianos","sells pianos"],"k":"B","sk":"Understands how the suffix -ist changes the meaning of a noun","b":"201–210","g":"VO"},{"n":38,"t":"mc","s":"The root **aqua** means “water.” An **aquatic** animal is one that —","o":["lives in water","lives in the desert","eats only plants","flies very high"],"k":"A","sk":"Applies knowledge of Greek or Latin roots in the 6-8 grade band","b":"201–210","g":"VO"},{"n":39,"t":"mc","s":"Which word suggests the MOST extreme anger?","o":["irritated","upset","annoyed","furious"],"k":"D","sk":"Analyzes nuances in meaning among related words","b":"201–210","g":"VO"},{"n":40,"t":"mc","s":"Read the sentence. “The hikers were **resilient**; even after the storm destroyed their camp, they rebuilt it and kept going.” What does **resilient** mean?","o":["easily frightened by storms","very careless","able to recover from difficulties","unable to move after the storm"],"k":"C","sk":"Uses context to determine the meaning of words in the 9-12 grade band","b":"211–220","g":"VO"},{"n":41,"t":"mc","s":"Read the paragraph. “When I asked my coach what he thought of my speech, he was **candid**. He told me directly that the beginning was boring and the ending was too long. It was hard to hear, but it helped.” What does **candid** mean?","o":["honest and direct","silent","polite but false","confused"],"k":"A","sk":"Uses paragraph-level context to determine word meaning","b":"211–220","g":"VO"},{"n":42,"t":"mc","s":"Which analogy is CORRECT? **Thermometer** is to **temperature** as **scale** is to —","o":["distance","colour","time","weight"],"k":"D","sk":"Uses analogies to understand word relationships","b":"211–220","g":"VO"},{"n":43,"t":"mc","s":"“After winning every match, the champion finally met his **Waterloo** in the final.” This is an allusion to a famous historical defeat. What does it mean?","o":["He swam across a river to celebrate.","He suffered a final, decisive defeat.","He visited a famous city after the final.","He won easily."],"k":"B","sk":"Interprets allusion and figurative language in context","b":"221–230","g":"VO"}]}]},{"id":"D","note":"Full practice test · 43 questions · harder","sections":[{"passage":{"title":"The Old Radio","genre":"story","paras":["The radio had sat on the top shelf of Grandfather’s cupboard for as long as I could remember: a heavy wooden box with a cracked dial and two brass knobs. When Grandfather died, my father carried it down and put it on my desk. “He wanted you to have it,” he said. “It hasn’t worked in thirty years.”","I did not want an old broken box. I wanted a new phone. But every evening, the radio sat there, watching me like a patient guest.","One weekend, out of boredom, I unscrewed the back. Inside was a forest of wires and small glass tubes. I searched online, borrowed a book from the library and asked the man at the electronics shop a hundred questions. He laughed and gave me an old part from a drawer for free.","It took me six weeks. On the last night, I turned the brass knob, and after a long hiss, a voice came through, faint and crackling, reading the evening news. My father appeared in the doorway. For a long time he did not speak. Then he said quietly, “That is exactly how it sounded when I was your age.”","I still have a phone. But the radio stays on my desk, and on some evenings, my father comes in, sits on the edge of my bed and listens with me."]},"items":[{"n":1,"t":"mc","s":"How does the narrator feel about the radio at first?","o":["afraid of it","proud to own something that had once belonged to his grandfather","excited to repair it as soon as possible","uninterested, because he would rather have a new phone"],"k":"D","sk":"Infers character feelings","b":"191–200","g":"LT"},{"n":2,"t":"mc","s":"“The radio sat there, watching me like a patient guest.” What does this simile suggest?","o":["The radio seems to be quietly waiting for the narrator’s attention.","The radio is broken and will never work again, no matter what he does.","A guest is staying in the narrator’s room.","The radio is very loud."],"k":"A","sk":"Interprets simile in context","b":"201–210","g":"LT"},{"n":3,"t":"mc","s":"Which detail BEST shows the narrator’s determination?","o":["He wants a new phone.","The radio is a heavy wooden box with a cracked dial and two brass knobs.","He searches online, borrows a book and asks many questions over six weeks.","His father carries the radio down from the top shelf."],"k":"C","sk":"Describes character traits; supporting evidence","b":"201–210","g":"LT"},{"n":4,"t":"mc","s":"Why does the father not speak for a long time when the radio works?","o":["He is angry about the loud, crackling noise the radio makes in the house.","He is moved by a sound that reminds him of his own childhood and his father.","He does not like the news.","He is surprised that the narrator is still awake."],"k":"B","sk":"Makes inferences from literary text","b":"211–220","g":"LT"},{"n":5,"t":"mc","s":"What theme does the story develop?","o":["Electronics shops are happy to give away free parts to young people.","New phones are more useful than old radios.","Repairs always take six weeks.","Objects from the past can connect generations of a family."],"k":"D","sk":"Analyzes the development of theme in literary text","b":"211–220","g":"LT"}]},{"passage":{"title":"Nutrition Facts: Date and Oat Bar","genre":"label","paras":["**Nutrition Facts** — Serving size: 1 bar (40 g) · Servings per box: 6","Amount per serving: Energy 150 calories · Protein 4 g · Total sugar 18 g · Fibre 3 g · Salt 0.1 g","*Ingredients: dates, oats, honey, almonds.* Contains nuts.","Tip: Health experts advise choosing snacks that are high in fibre and lower in sugar."]},"items":[{"n":6,"t":"mc","s":"How many bars are in one box?","o":["6","18","40","150"],"k":"A","sk":"Locates information in a text feature","b":"191–200","g":"IT"},{"n":7,"t":"mc","s":"Why does the label say “Contains nuts”?","o":["to show the price","to warn people who are allergic to nuts","to advertise the sweet taste of the bar","to explain how to cook the bar"],"k":"B","sk":"Determines specific purpose of a text feature","b":"201–210","g":"IT"},{"n":8,"t":"mc","s":"Ahmad eats two bars after school. How much protein does he get?","o":["4 g","6 g","8 g","12 g"],"k":"C","sk":"Analyzes quantitative information in informational text","b":"201–210","g":"IT"},{"n":9,"t":"mc","s":"How much total sugar is in the WHOLE box?","o":["18 g","24 g","90 g","108 g"],"k":"D","sk":"Analyzes quantitative information in informational text","b":"211–220","g":"IT"},{"n":10,"t":"mc","s":"Based on the tip, which change would make this bar a healthier snack?","o":["more sugar and less fibre","less sugar and more fibre","a bigger bar","more salt"],"k":"B","sk":"Integrates information from graphics and text","b":"211–220","g":"IT"}]},{"passage":{"title":"The Headline","genre":"drama","paras":["*(The school newspaper office. HISHAM, the editor, is pinning papers to a board. WAEL, a reporter, enters holding a notebook.)*","HISHAM: Wael! I have the perfect headline for Thursday: “Canteen to Close Forever!” Everyone will read it.","WAEL: *(frowning)* Is it true?","HISHAM: *(waving his hand)* Someone heard the manager say “closing.” That is enough.","WAEL: I went to ask him. *(He opens his notebook.)* The canteen will close for two days next week, to paint the walls.","HISHAM: *(pausing, then sitting down heavily)* Two days. That is not a headline.","WAEL: Maybe not an exciting one. But a newspaper that prints rumours will soon have readers who believe nothing it says.","HISHAM: *(slowly taking the paper down from the board)* Then write it: “Canteen Closed Two Days for Painting.” *(a small smile)* At least nobody will cry."]},"items":[{"n":11,"t":"mc","s":"What is the conflict in the scene?","o":["Hisham wants an exciting headline, but Wael insists on the truth.","Wael has lost the notebook with all of his interview notes inside.","The canteen manager is angry with the newspaper.","The newspaper office is being painted."],"k":"A","sk":"Identifies conflict in literary text","b":"201–210","g":"LT"},{"n":12,"t":"mc","s":"How are Hisham and Wael DIFFERENT?","o":["Hisham is a reporter; Wael is the editor of the paper.","Both want exciting headlines.","Hisham cares about attracting readers; Wael cares about accuracy.","Hisham checks every fact carefully; Wael likes to spread rumours."],"k":"C","sk":"Compares or contrasts characters","b":"201–210","g":"LT"},{"n":13,"t":"mc","s":"What is the theme of the scene?","o":["Truth is more important than attracting attention.","Painting walls takes two days.","Editors are always right about what readers want.","School canteens should never close, even for painting."],"k":"A","sk":"Determines theme in literary text","b":"201–210","g":"LT"},{"n":14,"t":"mc","s":"What does the stage direction *(waving his hand)* suggest about Hisham at that moment?","o":["He is greeting a friend who has entered the room.","He is very tired.","He does not think checking the facts is important.","He agrees with Wael that the facts must be checked."],"k":"C","sk":"Analyzes use of stage directions","b":"211–220","g":"LT"},{"n":15,"t":"mc","s":"What does Wael mean by “a newspaper that prints rumours will soon have readers who believe nothing it says”?","o":["Newspapers should not have headlines.","Readers do not like long stories in newspapers.","Rumours are always more exciting to read than the boring truth.","If the paper prints false stories, people will stop trusting it."],"k":"D","sk":"Analyzes dialogue to understand characters","b":"211–220","g":"LT"}]},{"passage":{"title":"Footprints","genre":"poem","paras":["I walked along the shore at dawn","And left my footprints, one by one;","The tide crept in on silver toes","And took them, as the water goes.","","I walked across the dunes at noon,","The wind erased them all too soon.","So now I wonder, as I go,","What marks of mine will others know?","","Not prints on sand, but deeds and words,","Like seeds that travel with the birds."],"poem":true},"items":[{"n":16,"t":"mc","s":"What happens to the speaker’s footprints in the first two stanzas?","o":["Other people follow them along the shore.","The tide and the wind erase them.","They become deeper.","They turn into seeds."],"k":"B","sk":"Locates details in poetry","b":"191–200","g":"LT"},{"n":17,"t":"mc","s":"“The tide crept in on silver toes.” What does this personification suggest?","o":["The tide was very loud.","The water came in quietly and gently.","The water was made of silver coins.","The tide went out quickly."],"k":"B","sk":"Interprets personification in context","b":"201–210","g":"LT"},{"n":18,"t":"mc","s":"What question does the speaker ask in the second stanza?","o":["what lasting marks he will leave on others","why the wind blows","how long the shore is from end to end","where the tide comes from each morning and evening"],"k":"A","sk":"Analyzes how specific parts of a poem contribute to meaning","b":"211–220","g":"LT"},{"n":19,"t":"mc","s":"“Like seeds that travel with the birds.” What does this simile suggest about good deeds and words?","o":["They belong only to birds.","They disappear quickly, like footprints in sand.","They are small and unimportant, like tiny seeds.","They can spread far and grow in other places."],"k":"D","sk":"Interprets simile in context","b":"211–220","g":"LT"},{"n":20,"t":"mc","s":"What is the theme of the poem?","o":["The wind is stronger and faster than the sea.","Footprints are easy to make.","Our actions and words leave a more lasting mark than anything physical.","The beach is a beautiful and peaceful place to walk at dawn, before the tide."],"k":"C","sk":"Determines theme in poetry","b":"211–220","g":"LT"}]},{"passage":{"title":"A Road Across the Sea","genre":"informational","paras":["For thousands of years, the only way to travel between the island of Bahrain and the Arabian mainland was by boat. Then, in 1986, the **King Fahd Causeway** opened. It is a road about 25 kilometres long that crosses the sea from Al-Khobar in Saudi Arabia to Bahrain.","The causeway is not one single bridge. It is made of several bridges joined by long embankments, which are raised roads built on rock and sand placed in the shallow sea. Halfway across, an artificial island holds the border stations of both countries, where travellers show their documents.","The causeway changed life in the region. Families on both sides can now visit each other easily, and many people cross for work, study and shopping. Trade between the two countries also grew, because trucks could carry goods directly instead of loading them onto ships.","Today, millions of people use the causeway every year. Because traffic often becomes heavy, plans have been made for new crossings, including a railway, to share the load."]},"items":[{"n":21,"t":"mc","s":"What is the central idea of the text?","o":["Bahrain is an island in the Gulf near the coast of Saudi Arabia, across from Al-Khobar.","Border stations on an island check travellers’ documents.","Trucks carry goods.","The King Fahd Causeway connected Bahrain and Saudi Arabia and changed life in the region."],"k":"D","sk":"Determines main/central idea in informational text","b":"201–210","g":"IT"},{"n":22,"t":"mc","s":"What are **embankments**, according to the text?","o":["tall towers that hold up the bridges over the sea","tunnels built under the water","raised roads built on rock and sand in the sea","boats that carry cars"],"k":"C","sk":"Determines domain-specific word meaning in context","b":"201–210","g":"IT"},{"n":23,"t":"mc","s":"Why did trade grow after the causeway opened?","o":["Trucks could carry goods directly instead of loading them onto ships.","Bahrain and the mainland moved closer together.","People stopped shopping.","Boats became much more expensive to use after the causeway opened in 1986."],"k":"A","sk":"Determines the cause of a situation","b":"201–210","g":"IT"},{"n":24,"t":"mc","s":"How is the text organized?","o":["It compares the island of Bahrain with another island in the Gulf and a city on the mainland.","It describes the causeway, explains its effects and then looks to the future.","It gives the steps that engineers followed to build the bridges.","It tells a story about one traveller."],"k":"B","sk":"Describes the organizational structure of a text","b":"211–220","g":"IT"},{"n":25,"t":"mc","s":"What can the reader infer from the last paragraph?","o":["Very few people use the causeway these days.","The causeway has become so popular that more crossings are needed.","The causeway will soon close because it is too old and too narrow for cars.","The railway is already finished."],"k":"B","sk":"Makes inferences from informational text","b":"211–220","g":"IT"}]},{"passage":{"title":"It Is Time to Say Goodbye to Plastic Bags","genre":"argument","paras":["Every week, my family brings home dozens of plastic bags from the supermarket. Most are used for only a few minutes, yet they can stay in the environment for hundreds of years. Shops should stop giving out single-use plastic bags.","Plastic bags cause serious harm to wildlife. Sea turtles, camels and seabirds can mistake them for food, and an animal with a stomach full of plastic cannot eat properly. Bags that blow into the desert or the sea do not simply disappear; they break into tiny pieces that remain for a very long time.","Other countries have already acted. Well over one hundred countries have passed laws to limit or ban plastic bags, which shows that change is possible.","Some shoppers say that bags are convenient and that reusable bags cost money. It is true that a reusable bag costs more than a free plastic one. But a strong cloth bag can be used hundreds of times, so over a year it saves money and reduces waste.","Plastic bags are the most terrible invention in human history. If we act now, our beaches and deserts can be clean again."]},"items":[{"n":26,"t":"mc","s":"What is the author’s claim?","o":["Supermarkets are too busy and crowded, especially at weekends.","Cloth bags are too expensive for most families.","Turtles eat plastic.","Shops should stop giving out single-use plastic bags."],"k":"D","sk":"Determines author’s main argument or claim","b":"201–210","g":"IT"},{"n":27,"t":"mc","s":"Which reason is supported by the information about turtles, camels and seabirds?","o":["Plastic bags harm wildlife.","Plastic bags are convenient.","Other countries have passed laws.","Reusable bags save money."],"k":"A","sk":"Identifies reasons that support a claim","b":"201–210","g":"IT"},{"n":28,"t":"mc","s":"How does the author respond to the counterclaim about cost?","o":["by saying that plastic bags are actually more expensive than cloth bags over a long time","by ignoring the counterclaim","by admitting reusable bags cost more but explaining they save money over time","by agreeing that plastic bags are better for shoppers in every way"],"k":"C","sk":"Identifies a counterclaim and rebuttal","b":"211–220","g":"IT"},{"n":29,"t":"mc","s":"Why does the author mention that over one hundred countries have passed laws?","o":["to describe the supermarket","to show that limiting plastic bags is possible","to show that plastic bags are popular around the world","to explain how plastic bags are made in factories"],"k":"B","sk":"Evaluates evidence used to support claims","b":"211–220","g":"IT"},{"n":30,"t":"mc","s":"Which sentence is an exaggerated claim that weakens the argument?","o":["Plastic bags are the most terrible invention in human history.","A strong cloth bag can be used hundreds of times.","Well over one hundred countries have passed laws.","Most are used for only a few minutes, yet they can stay in the environment for hundreds of years."],"k":"A","sk":"Analyzes inadequately supported claims","b":"221–230","g":"IT"}]},{"passage":null,"items":[{"n":31,"t":"mc","s":"The root **therm** means “heat.” What does a **thermos** flask do?","o":["records sound","makes light","measures the distance you travel","keeps drinks hot or cold"],"k":"D","sk":"Applies knowledge of Greek or Latin roots in the 6-8 grade band","b":"201–210","g":"VO"},{"n":32,"t":"mc","s":"“My little brother is a walking dictionary.” What does this metaphor suggest?","o":["He carries a dictionary everywhere.","He cannot read.","He knows the meanings of many words.","He walks very slowly."],"k":"C","sk":"Interprets metaphor in context","b":"201–210","g":"VO"},{"n":33,"t":"mc","s":"In which sentence does **current** mean “a flow of water”?","o":["She is the current captain of the school team.","The current price is too high.","The swimmer was pulled away by a strong current.","Please read the current news about the weather."],"k":"C","sk":"Determines which meaning of a multiple-meaning word fits","b":"201–210","g":"VO"},{"n":34,"t":"mc","s":"The root **rupt** means “break.” What does it mean to **disrupt** a lesson?","o":["to break its normal flow","to repeat it","to end it politely","to start it on time every morning"],"k":"A","sk":"Applies knowledge of Greek or Latin roots in the 6-8 grade band","b":"201–210","g":"VO"},{"n":35,"t":"mc","s":"“Saad’s bedroom was so messy that it looked as if a hurricane had passed through.” This is an example of —","o":["a fact","hyperbole","an allusion","onomatopoeia"],"k":"B","sk":"Interprets hyperbole in context","b":"201–210","g":"VO"},{"n":36,"t":"mc","s":"The prefix **trans-** means “across.” What does **transatlantic** mean?","o":["near the Atlantic coast","under the Atlantic Ocean","without the Atlantic Ocean","crossing the Atlantic Ocean"],"k":"D","sk":"Uses a defined root and/or affix to determine word meaning","b":"201–210","g":"VO"},{"n":37,"t":"mc","s":"“The team’s new striker was their **secret weapon**.” What does this suggest?","o":["He was a secret agent on the team.","He did not play.","He was an unexpected advantage.","He carried a weapon."],"k":"C","sk":"Interprets figurative language in context","b":"201–210","g":"VO"},{"n":38,"t":"mc","s":"Read the paragraph. “The explorers were **intrepid**. They crossed frozen rivers, climbed icy cliffs and slept in the open during snowstorms, yet they never thought of turning back.” What does **intrepid** mean?","o":["foolish","fearless","lazy","lost"],"k":"B","sk":"Uses paragraph-level context to determine word meaning","b":"211–220","g":"VO"},{"n":39,"t":"mc","s":"Which word suggests the MOST careful kind of looking?","o":["scrutinize","look around","glance","peek"],"k":"A","sk":"Analyzes nuances in meaning among related words","b":"211–220","g":"VO"},{"n":40,"t":"mc","s":"Read the sentence. “The teacher asked us to **infer** the character’s feelings from her actions.” What does **infer** mean?","o":["to guess without any reason","to forget","to copy exactly","to work out from clues"],"k":"D","sk":"Determines the meaning of academic words in context","b":"211–220","g":"VO"},{"n":41,"t":"mc","s":"Which word BEST completes the sentence? “The weather forecast predicted sunshine; ___, it rained all afternoon.”","o":["nonetheless","likewise","therefore","for instance"],"k":"A","sk":"Identifies words in context that show a contrast","b":"211–220","g":"VO"},{"n":42,"t":"mc","s":"Which word means the OPPOSITE of **transparent**?","o":["clear","glassy","bright","opaque"],"k":"D","sk":"Identifies antonyms","b":"211–220","g":"VO"},{"n":43,"t":"mc","s":"Read the sentence. “The pilot had to **abort** the landing because a truck was on the runway.” What does **abort** mean?","o":["to repeat","to stop before finishing","to speed up","to complete the landing"],"k":"B","sk":"Uses context to determine word meaning","b":"211–220","g":"VO"}]}]},{"id":"E","note":"Final full practice test · 43 questions · hardest","sections":[{"passage":{"title":"Ibn al-Haytham and the Science of Light","genre":"informational","paras":["For many centuries, some thinkers believed that we see because our eyes send out rays that touch objects. About a thousand years ago, a scholar named **Ibn al-Haytham**, who was born in Basra and later worked in Cairo, showed that the opposite is true: we see because light travels from objects into our eyes.","What made Ibn al-Haytham’s work so important was not only his conclusion but also his method. Instead of simply accepting earlier ideas, he tested them with careful experiments. He passed light through small holes into dark rooms, studied how it travelled in straight lines and recorded what happened. He then checked whether his results could be repeated.","His *Book of Optics* explained how light reflects off mirrors and bends as it passes through water and glass. He also described the **camera obscura**, a dark room with a tiny hole in one wall, through which an upside-down image of the outside world appears on the opposite wall.","Because he relied on observation and testing, many historians consider Ibn al-Haytham one of the early pioneers of the scientific method. His book was later translated into Latin and influenced scientists in Europe for centuries."]},"items":[{"n":1,"t":"mc","s":"Which idea did Ibn al-Haytham show to be wrong?","o":["that our eyes send out rays that touch objects","that light reflects off mirrors","that light bends in water","that light travels in straight lines through the air"],"k":"A","sk":"Locates details in informational text","b":"201–210","g":"IT"},{"n":2,"t":"mc","s":"What is the central idea of the text?","o":["Mirrors reflect light.","His book was later translated into Latin and read in Europe.","Ibn al-Haytham changed the understanding of light and helped develop the scientific method.","Ibn al-Haytham was born in Basra and later worked in Cairo, where he wrote his books on light."],"k":"C","sk":"Determines main/central idea in informational text","b":"211–220","g":"IT"},{"n":3,"t":"mc","s":"According to the second paragraph, why was his METHOD important?","o":["He worked only at night.","He tested ideas with experiments and checked that results could be repeated.","He accepted earlier ideas without testing them, which saved him a lot of time.","He wrote very long books that explained everything about light."],"k":"B","sk":"Analyzes how specific paragraphs contribute to meaning","b":"211–220","g":"IT"},{"n":4,"t":"mc","s":"What is a **camera obscura**, as described in the text?","o":["a mirror that reflects the light of the stars into a room","a modern digital camera that takes very clear photographs of the night sky","a book about light","a dark room where light through a tiny hole forms an upside-down image"],"k":"D","sk":"Determines domain-specific word meaning in context","b":"211–220","g":"IT"},{"n":5,"t":"mc","s":"What is the author’s attitude toward Ibn al-Haytham?","o":["respectful admiration","doubt","strong criticism and doubt","amusement"],"k":"A","sk":"Determines author’s attitude in informational text","b":"211–220","g":"IT"}]},{"passage":{"title":"The Photograph","genre":"story","paras":["I found the photograph while looking for my passport in the drawer of my father’s desk. A thin boy of about my age stood on a running track, holding a silver cup above his head. On the back, in faded blue ink, someone had written: *Regional Champion, 1500 m.*","I knew that face. It was my father’s.","At dinner, I put the photograph beside his plate. “You never told me,” I said. He looked at it for a long moment, and his eyes went somewhere far away.","He was fifteen, he said, when the coach told him he could run for the national team. That same year, his father fell ill, and someone had to help run the family shop. “I kept the cup in the shop for a while,” he said, smiling a little. “Customers used it to hold pens.”","“Do you regret it?” I asked.","He thought about the question honestly. “Sometimes,” he said. “But because of that shop, your uncle studied medicine, your aunt became a teacher, and you,” he tapped the table, “have never once worried about where your next meal would come from.”","The next morning, I found the photograph in a frame on the living room wall. And when I went out for my run, my father was standing at the door in his old trainers. “Wait for me,” he said."]},"items":[{"n":6,"t":"mc","s":"From whose point of view is the story told?","o":["an outside narrator who knows everyone’s thoughts","the son’s, in the first person","the father’s, in the first person","the coach’s"],"k":"B","sk":"Recognizes first-person point of view","b":"201–210","g":"LT"},{"n":7,"t":"mc","s":"“His eyes went somewhere far away.” What does this suggest?","o":["The father cannot see well.","The father is looking out of the window.","The father is remembering his past.","The father is angry."],"k":"C","sk":"Determines the meaning of a nonliteral phrase","b":"201–210","g":"LT"},{"n":8,"t":"mc","s":"How does the author reveal the father’s past?","o":["through an old newspaper article about the race","through a dream","through a letter that the coach sent to the family many years ago","through a photograph that leads to the father telling his story"],"k":"D","sk":"Analyzes how an author develops a character’s background","b":"211–220","g":"LT"},{"n":9,"t":"mc","s":"What does the detail that customers used the cup “to hold pens” show?","o":["The father did not really like running.","The shop sold pens.","The cup was very large and could hold many pens and pencils.","The father put his family’s needs before his own achievement."],"k":"D","sk":"Interprets symbolism in literary text","b":"221–230","g":"LT"},{"n":10,"t":"mc","s":"Which theme does the story develop?","o":["Running is the best sport for young people to try, even if they are busy.","Shops are better than sports.","Personal sacrifice for family can bring a quiet kind of pride.","Old photographs should stay hidden in drawers."],"k":"C","sk":"Analyzes the development of theme in literary text","b":"221–230","g":"LT"}]},{"passage":{"title":"The Watcher of the Sky","genre":"drama","paras":["*(The roof of an observatory in Baghdad, long ago. Night. MASTER THABIT bends over a large brass instrument. His apprentice, ZAID, paces impatiently.)*","ZAID: Master, we have measured the same stars for forty nights. When will we discover something new?","THABIT: *(without looking up)* Write the position of the bright star near the horizon.","ZAID: *(sighing as he writes)* It is exactly where it was yesterday.","THABIT: Good. Now we know that it is a star. A thing that does not move teaches us how to see the things that do.","*(ZAID looks up. Near the edge of the sky, a faint, fuzzy light with a pale tail glows where there was nothing the night before. THABIT has not yet noticed it.)*","ZAID: *(whispering)* Master… was that light there last night?","THABIT: *(turning slowly, then checking the notebook carefully)* No. It was not. *(He smiles.)* Forty nights of “nothing new,” Zaid, and that is how we know this is."]},"items":[{"n":11,"t":"mc","s":"What is Zaid’s problem at the beginning?","o":["He is impatient because their work seems repetitive.","He has lost the notebook with all the records.","He has broken the brass instrument by accident while measuring.","He cannot see the stars."],"k":"A","sk":"Identifies conflict in literary text","b":"201–210","g":"LT"},{"n":12,"t":"mc","s":"What does Thabit mean by “A thing that does not move teaches us how to see the things that do”?","o":["Only moving things in the sky are worth studying.","Knowing what stays the same helps us notice what changes.","Stars are not important to study because they never move at all.","Zaid should stop moving."],"k":"B","sk":"Determines the meaning of a nonliteral phrase","b":"211–220","g":"LT"},{"n":13,"t":"mc","s":"Why does Thabit check the notebook before answering?","o":["to write a poem","to hide the discovery from his apprentice","to confirm with recorded evidence that the light is new","to check the time so that he knows when to go down to bed"],"k":"C","sk":"Explains character motivation","b":"211–220","g":"LT"},{"n":14,"t":"mc","s":"The stage directions say that Thabit “has not yet noticed” the new light. What effect does this create?","o":["It shows that the new light is not real at all.","It shows that Thabit has fallen asleep at his work on the observatory roof.","It explains how telescopes work.","The audience knows something before Thabit does, which builds suspense."],"k":"D","sk":"Analyzes dramatic irony","b":"221–230","g":"LT"},{"n":15,"t":"mc","s":"How has Zaid’s view of the “boring” observations changed by the end?","o":["He now believes that all the stars move every night.","He sees that the careful records made the discovery possible.","He decides to leave the observatory.","He still thinks that the forty nights were a complete waste of time."],"k":"B","sk":"Understands how characters are developed or changed","b":"221–230","g":"LT"}]},{"passage":{"title":"The Desert Is a Book","genre":"poem","paras":["The desert is a book that few can read;","Its pages are the dunes, its ink the wind.","Each morning writes new lines in rippled sand,","Each night the stars bend low to turn the page.","","The careless traveller sees only blank and gold,","An empty chapter with no words at all.","But Bedouin eyes can read the lizard’s trail,","The rain-sign in a cloud, the camel’s path.","","So learn the language of the open land:","The book is full. The reader must be patient."],"poem":true},"items":[{"n":16,"t":"mc","s":"In the metaphor, what are the “pages” and the “ink”?","o":["the dunes and the wind","the lizard and the camel","the clouds and the rain","the stars and the night"],"k":"A","sk":"Interprets metaphor in context","b":"201–210","g":"LT"},{"n":17,"t":"mc","s":"What is the extended metaphor in the poem?","o":["The wind is compared to a camel.","The desert is compared to a book.","The sand is compared to water.","The stars are compared to travellers."],"k":"B","sk":"Interprets extended metaphor","b":"211–220","g":"LT"},{"n":18,"t":"mc","s":"How are the “careless traveller” and the Bedouin DIFFERENT?","o":["The traveller sees nothing, but the Bedouin notices signs everywhere.","The traveller can read the desert easily, but the Bedouin cannot read it.","Both follow the camel’s path.","Both see an empty desert."],"k":"A","sk":"Compares and contrasts details in literary text","b":"211–220","g":"LT"},{"n":19,"t":"mc","s":"“Each night the stars bend low to turn the page.” What does this line suggest?","o":["The desert has no nights.","The stars fall down to the ground every night.","The traveller reads a book by starlight each night.","Each night brings a change, as if starting a new page."],"k":"D","sk":"Analyzes the effect of figurative language","b":"221–230","g":"LT"},{"n":20,"t":"mc","s":"What is the theme of the poem?","o":["Books should be read quickly.","Deserts are empty places with nothing to see or learn, so travellers rush through.","The natural world is full of meaning for those who observe it patiently.","Travellers should avoid the desert because it is dangerous."],"k":"C","sk":"Determines theme in poetry","b":"221–230","g":"LT"}]},{"passage":{"title":"When Coral Turns White","genre":"informational","paras":["Coral reefs look like colourful rocks, but each coral is made of thousands of tiny animals. Inside their bodies live even tinier algae. The algae make food from sunlight and share it with the coral, and they also give the coral its bright colours.","When sea water becomes too warm for too long, this partnership breaks down. The stressed coral pushes the algae out of its body. Without the algae, the coral loses its colour and becomes white. This is called **coral bleaching**.","Bleached coral is not dead, but it is starving. If the water cools again soon, the algae can return and the coral may recover. If the heat lasts too long, the coral may die, and the many fish and other creatures that depend on the reef lose their home.","Scientists have found some hope in the northern Red Sea. Corals in this area seem able to survive higher temperatures than many corals elsewhere. Researchers are studying them to understand how other reefs might be protected as the oceans warm."]},"items":[{"n":21,"t":"mc","s":"What is the relationship between coral and algae?","o":["The coral eats the algae when there is no other food.","The algae harm the coral by taking all of its food and giving nothing back.","They have no connection.","They help each other: the algae share food and the coral gives them a home."],"k":"D","sk":"Analyzes implicit relationships between ideas","b":"211–220","g":"IT"},{"n":22,"t":"mc","s":"What causes coral bleaching?","o":["Sea water stays too warm for too long, so the coral pushes out the algae.","Divers paint the coral.","Fish eat the coral’s bright colour when they cannot find any other food.","Strong sunlight turns the coral white in a single day."],"k":"A","sk":"Determines the cause of a situation","b":"211–220","g":"IT"},{"n":23,"t":"mc","s":"According to the text, which statement is TRUE about bleached coral?","o":["It is always dead and can never recover.","It grows faster.","It can recover if the water cools soon.","It becomes more colourful."],"k":"C","sk":"Identifies evidence that supports a statement","b":"211–220","g":"IT"},{"n":24,"t":"mc","s":"Why are scientists interested in the corals of the northern Red Sea?","o":["They are easy to collect and sell to aquariums.","They seem able to survive higher temperatures, which may help protect other reefs.","They are the most colourful corals found anywhere in the world’s oceans and seas today.","They have no algae."],"k":"B","sk":"Determines details that support a stated idea","b":"211–220","g":"IT"},{"n":25,"t":"mc","s":"How does the last paragraph change the tone of the text?","o":["It moves from describing a problem to offering some hope.","It becomes more frightening by describing dead reefs along the coast.","It repeats the information in the first paragraph.","It becomes humorous."],"k":"A","sk":"Analyzes how specific paragraphs contribute to meaning","b":"221–230","g":"IT"}]},{"passage":{"title":"Is Space Exploration Worth the Cost?","genre":"viewpoints","paras":["**Viewpoint 1 — Dr. Lina, space scientist:** Space exploration improves life on Earth. Satellites help forecast storms, guide ships and planes, and connect phones across the world. Technology first developed for space programs has also led to better medical equipment and water filters. When we explore space, we solve problems that help everyone.","**Viewpoint 2 — Mr. Fares, newspaper writer:** Space programs cost billions. Meanwhile, millions of people on Earth still lack clean water and good schools. Last year I visited a village where children walked two hours to fetch water, and I could not stop thinking about how much one rocket costs. We should solve the problems in front of us before we reach for the stars."]},"items":[{"n":26,"t":"mc","s":"What is Dr. Lina’s main claim?","o":["Satellites are dangerous.","Medical equipment is expensive.","Space programs cost far too much money.","Space exploration improves life on Earth."],"k":"D","sk":"Determines author’s main argument or claim","b":"201–210","g":"IT"},{"n":27,"t":"mc","s":"What does Mr. Fares mean by “before we reach for the stars”?","o":["before building tall buildings","before becoming famous","before spending money on space exploration","before looking up at the stars in the night sky"],"k":"C","sk":"Determines the meaning of a nonliteral phrase","b":"211–220","g":"IT"},{"n":28,"t":"mc","s":"What kind of evidence does Mr. Fares use that Dr. Lina does NOT?","o":["facts about storms","a personal story about a village he visited","information about how satellites help people","examples of technology developed for space"],"k":"B","sk":"Evaluates evidence used to support claims","b":"221–230","g":"IT"},{"n":29,"t":"mc","s":"On which point would BOTH writers MOST LIKELY agree?","o":["Problems on Earth matter and should be solved.","Rockets are cheap.","Space programs should be stopped at once by all countries.","Satellites are useless."],"k":"A","sk":"Compares and contrasts viewpoints in multiple texts","b":"221–230","g":"IT"},{"n":30,"t":"mc","s":"Which detail from Dr. Lina could BEST answer Mr. Fares’s concern about clean water?","o":["Satellites connect phones.","Technology first developed for space has led to better water filters.","Satellites help guide ships and planes safely across the world every day.","When we explore space, we solve problems that help everyone."],"k":"B","sk":"Synthesizes information found in multiple texts","b":"221–230","g":"IT"}]},{"passage":null,"items":[{"n":31,"t":"mc","s":"“The news hit him like a ton of bricks.” What does this mean?","o":["A ton of bricks fell on him from the roof.","He was building a house.","The news was boring.","The news shocked him very strongly."],"k":"D","sk":"Interprets simile in context","b":"201–210","g":"VO"},{"n":32,"t":"mc","s":"The root **chron** means “time.” A **chronic** problem is one that —","o":["is easy to solve","happens only once in a while","lasts for a long time","is very small"],"k":"C","sk":"Applies knowledge of Greek or Latin roots in the 6-8 grade band","b":"211–220","g":"VO"},{"n":33,"t":"mc","s":"Read the sentence. “The scientist’s **hypothesis** was that plants grow faster with music; she tested it for six weeks.” What is a **hypothesis**?","o":["a kind of plant","an idea that can be tested","a final law that has been proven","a type of music"],"k":"B","sk":"Determines the meaning of academic words in context","b":"211–220","g":"VO"},{"n":34,"t":"mc","s":"The prefix **bene-** means “good,” and **mal-** means “bad.” A **malfunction** is —","o":["a very good result","a new invention","a kind of celebration for a new machine","a failure to work properly"],"k":"D","sk":"Uses a defined root and/or affix to determine word meaning","b":"211–220","g":"VO"},{"n":35,"t":"mc","s":"“Opportunity knocked on Majed’s door when the coach invited him to join the team.” What does this personification mean?","o":["A good chance came to Majed.","Someone visited Majed’s house.","Majed heard a loud noise.","The coach broke the door."],"k":"A","sk":"Interprets personification in context","b":"211–220","g":"VO"},{"n":36,"t":"mc","s":"Which word means the OPPOSITE of **diligent**?","o":["careful","busy","lazy","hard-working"],"k":"C","sk":"Identifies antonyms","b":"211–220","g":"VO"},{"n":37,"t":"mc","s":"Read the sentence. “The two friends had a **mutual** respect; each admired the other.” What does **mutual** mean?","o":["secret","new","shared by both","one-sided and unfair"],"k":"C","sk":"Uses context to determine word meaning","b":"211–220","g":"VO"},{"n":38,"t":"mc","s":"Which analogy is CORRECT? **Author** is to **book** as **composer** is to —","o":["building","garden","painting","music"],"k":"D","sk":"Uses analogies to understand word relationships","b":"211–220","g":"VO"},{"n":39,"t":"mc","s":"Read the paragraph. “At first the plan seemed **feasible**. We had enough money, enough time and enough volunteers. Only later did we discover that the hall was already booked.” What does **feasible** mean?","o":["possible to do","too expensive","very exciting","already finished"],"k":"A","sk":"Uses paragraph-level context to determine word meaning","b":"221–230","g":"VO"},{"n":40,"t":"mc","s":"Which word suggests the MOST serious kind of mistake?","o":["slip","blunder","error","mistake"],"k":"B","sk":"Analyzes nuances in meaning among related words","b":"221–230","g":"VO"},{"n":41,"t":"mc","s":"“Opening that old box was like opening Pandora’s box.” This allusion to a Greek myth suggests that opening the box —","o":["was very easy","brought great treasure and riches","caused many unexpected problems","made everyone laugh"],"k":"C","sk":"Interprets allusion and figurative language in context","b":"221–230","g":"VO"},{"n":42,"t":"mc","s":"Which word BEST completes the sentence? “The evidence was weak; ___, the committee could not reach a decision.”","o":["accordingly","similarly","meanwhile","however"],"k":"A","sk":"Words that show a cause-effect relationship","b":"221–230","g":"VO"},{"n":43,"t":"mc","s":"In which sentence does **grave** mean “very serious”?","o":["He wrote a grave accent on the letter.","The doctor gave a grave warning about the illness.","Flowers were placed beside the grave.","They visited the grave of a famous poet in the old town."],"k":"B","sk":"Determines which meaning of a multiple-meaning word fits","b":"221–230","g":"VO"}]}]}],"pass":[{"title":"The Potter’s Apprentice","genre":"story","paras":["Hamza had worked in Master Idris’s pottery shop for a year, and he was proud of his speed. He could shape ten jugs in the time it took the old master to shape three.","One morning, a merchant ordered fifty jugs for a wedding. “I will finish them by tonight,” Hamza promised, and he worked faster than ever. By evening, fifty jugs stood drying in the sun.","The next day, the merchant returned, angry. Water was leaking from half of the jugs. Hamza’s face burned. He waited for Master Idris to scold him, but the old man only picked up one cracked jug and turned it in his hands.","“Clay remembers every hurry,” he said quietly. “A jug is not finished when it looks finished. It is finished when it holds water.”","That week, Hamza remade the jugs. He wedged the clay longer, shaped the walls more evenly and let them dry slowly in the shade. It took him four days instead of one. When the merchant filled the new jugs, not a single drop escaped.","Years later, people travelled from other towns to buy Hamza’s pottery. When young apprentices asked for his secret, he smiled and told them the same thing every time: “Clay remembers every hurry.”"]},{"title":"The Calligrapher’s Brush","genre":"story","paras":["Yazeed wanted to win the school calligraphy competition more than anything. He had two weeks to copy a line of poetry in perfect Thuluth script, and he practised every evening at the table of Master Idris, an old calligrapher who lived above the bookshop.","On the first evening, Yazeed wrote the whole line in ten minutes and held it up proudly. Master Idris looked at it for a long time. “Your letters are running,” he said. “A letter that runs cannot breathe.” He handed Yazeed a fresh sheet. “One letter tonight. Only one.”","Yazeed thought this was a waste of time, but he obeyed. Night after night, he wrote one letter, then two, then a single word. His hand slowed down. He began to notice how the reed pen thinned and thickened, how the space between letters mattered as much as the letters themselves.","Three days before the competition, Yazeed’s little brother knocked a cup of tea across his nearly finished page. Yazeed stared at the brown stain spreading through the ink. A month earlier he would have shouted. Instead he took a deep breath, pulled out a new sheet and began again, one letter at a time.","He finished the new page the night before the competition. He did not win first prize; he came second. But when Master Idris saw the page, he nodded slowly. “Now,” he said, “your letters breathe.”"]},{"title":"Catching Water from the Air","genre":"informational","paras":["In some dry coastal places, it almost never rains, yet the air is full of water. Thick fog rolls in from the sea most mornings, but it disappears before people can use it. For villages where clean water is scarce, this is a serious problem.","Engineers have designed a simple solution: the fog net. A fog net is a large panel of fine plastic mesh stretched between two poles. When fog blows through the mesh, tiny droplets stick to the threads. The droplets join together, become heavy and run down into a pipe at the bottom.","A single large net can collect hundreds of litres of water on a foggy day. The water is clean enough for drinking after simple filtering, and it can also water gardens and trees.","Fog nets have limits. They work only where fog is common, and strong winds can damage them. Still, in places such as the coasts of Chile and Morocco, villages now use this device to collect water that once floated away. Scientists are also testing new mesh shapes that copy the bumpy backs of desert beetles, which collect water from fog in a similar way."]},{"title":"Holding Back the Sand","genre":"informational","paras":["In many dry parts of the world, land that once grew grass and shrubs is slowly turning into desert. This process is called **desertification**. It is a serious problem because people and animals lose the land they depend on for food.","Desertification has several causes. When too many animals graze in one place, they eat plants faster than the plants can grow back. When trees and shrubs are cut down for fuel, their roots no longer hold the soil in place. Without plants, the wind blows the topsoil away, and long droughts make the damage worse.","Scientists and governments are working on solutions. One is to plant trees and native shrubs that can survive with little water; their roots hold the soil, and their leaves give shade. Another is to control grazing so that land can rest and recover. Farmers are also learning to collect rainwater and water crops more carefully.","Saudi Arabia has joined this effort. Through the Saudi Green Initiative, the country has announced a goal of planting ten billion trees within its borders in the coming decades and restoring large areas of damaged land."]},{"title":"Roots, Shades and Clues","genre":"sentences","paras":["**Roots:** chron = time · therm = heat · scrib/script = write · vis/vid = see · rupt = break · photo = light","**Nuance:** *glance*, *stare* and *glare* all mean “look,” but *glare* adds anger and *stare* adds a long time.","**Allusion:** a short reference to a well-known story or person. Calling a hard-working person “a real Ibn Battuta” compares him to the famous traveller."]},{"title":"Lost in the Empty Quarter","genre":"story","paras":["The sun had gone down an hour ago, and I could no longer see the tyre tracks. The dunes around me rose like frozen waves, silver in the moonlight, and every one looked exactly like the last.","I had wandered from the camp to photograph a fox, and now the desert was silent in a way that made my heart thump. No wind. No voices. Only the soft hiss of sand sliding down a slope behind me. The silence felt eerie, as if the desert were holding its breath.","I remembered what my uncle had taught me: “When you are lost, stop. Look up.” I found the bright star he called the North Star and remembered that the camp was to the south, the way we had driven in. I turned my back to the star and began to walk, counting my steps to stay calm.","After what felt like hours, I saw it: a small orange glow on the horizon, a campfire flickering like a beacon. My legs, so heavy a moment earlier, suddenly felt light. When my uncle wrapped a blanket around my shoulders, I understood that the desert had not been my enemy. I had simply needed to slow down and read it."]},{"title":"The Storm Over the Gulf","genre":"story","paras":["The sky over the Gulf had been a clear, polished blue all morning. Sultan sat at the back of his father’s small fishing boat, pulling in the net, while his father checked the engine and hummed an old sailor’s song.","By early afternoon, the song stopped. Sultan’s father was staring at the horizon, where a bruise-coloured line of cloud was growing wider. The wind changed direction and grew colder. Small waves began to slap against the side of the boat like impatient hands.","Within minutes, the sea turned grey and wild. Rain hammered the deck so hard that Sultan could not see the shore. The boat rose on each wave and then dropped sharply, and every drop made his stomach twist. His father’s voice cut through the roar: “Hold the rope! Keep your eyes on me!”","Sultan wrapped the rope around his wrists and fixed his eyes on his father’s steady hands on the wheel. Slowly, carefully, his father turned the boat so that it met each wave head-on. After what felt like hours, the rain thinned, and the dark shape of the harbour wall appeared.","That night, Sultan could still feel the boat rising and falling as he lay in bed. But he also remembered his father’s hands on the wheel, calm in the middle of the storm, and he knew that he would want to go to sea again."]},{"title":"Every School Needs a Garden","genre":"argument","paras":["Every school in our city should create a vegetable garden that students plant and care for themselves. A school garden is not a luxury; it is a classroom without walls.","Gardens improve learning. Measuring plant growth teaches mathematics, and studying soil and insects teaches science. In a study of 300 students, those who took part in a school garden program scored higher on science tests than students who did not.","Gardens also change how students eat. When children grow vegetables themselves, they are far more willing to taste them. At one school in Riyadh, the number of students choosing salad at lunch doubled in the year after its garden opened.","Some people argue that gardens cost too much and need water in a hot country. This concern is reasonable, but drip irrigation uses very little water, and many companies are happy to donate seeds and tools.","The question is not whether we can afford school gardens. It is whether we can afford to keep our students indoors, away from the living world they are learning about."]},{"title":"Every Child Should Learn to Swim","genre":"argument","paras":["Swimming is not just a sport; it is a life skill. I believe every school should teach all students to swim before they finish elementary school.","The most important reason is safety. According to the World Health Organization, drowning is one of the leading causes of injury-related death for children around the world. A child who can swim, float and stay calm in water is far more likely to survive an accident at the beach or a pool.","Swimming also builds health. It exercises the whole body, strengthens the heart and lungs, and is gentle on the joints, so almost anyone can do it.","Some people argue that swimming lessons are too expensive for schools, because pools cost a lot to build and run. This is a fair concern. However, schools do not need their own pools; they can partner with public pools and clubs, as many cities already do. The cost of a few lessons is tiny compared with the value of a child’s life.","Finally, everyone knows that children who swim get better marks in every subject. It is time to make swimming lessons a requirement."]},{"title":"Text 1: Diary of a Climber · Text 2: The Challenges of Everest","genre":"paired","paras":["**Text 1: Diary of a Climber (fiction)**","Day 38. We are camped at 7,900 metres. Every step feels like lifting a sack of rice, and the air is so thin that I count my breaths. Tonight the wind screams against the tent. Tomorrow we try for the summit. I am frightened, but I think of my father, who never finished his climb, and I know I must try.","**Text 2: The Challenges of Everest (article)**","Mount Everest is 8,849 metres high, the tallest mountain above sea level. At the summit, the air has only about one-third as much oxygen as at sea level, so most climbers use bottled oxygen. Climbers spend weeks moving between camps to acclimatize. In 2013, Raha Moharrak became the first Saudi woman to reach the summit."]},{"title":"Text 1: The Night the Wadi Ran · Text 2: Flash Floods in the Desert","genre":"paired","paras":["**Text 1: The Night the Wadi Ran (a narrative)**","We had driven past the dry valley a hundred times. That evening, after an hour of heavy rain in the mountains, my uncle stopped the car at its edge. The wadi was no longer dry. Brown water roared through it, carrying branches and stones, as fast as a river in a hurry. A pickup truck on the other side had tried to cross and now lay tipped on its side. Its driver stood on the bank, soaked and silent. My uncle turned the car around without a word. “The desert keeps its rivers hidden,” he said at last, “until it decides to show them.”","**Text 2: Flash Floods in the Desert (an article)**","In desert regions, most of the year passes without rain. When a storm does come, the hard, dry ground cannot absorb the water quickly, so it runs across the surface and gathers in valleys called wadis. Within minutes, a dry wadi can become a fast-moving flood. These sudden floods are called **flash floods**. They are especially dangerous because the rain may fall far away, so people in the valley may not even see a cloud. Moving water only about 30 centimetres deep can carry away a small car. Safety experts give one clear rule: never try to drive or walk across a flooded wadi."]}]};
(function(d){d.units.forEach(function(u){u.sets.forEach(function(s){s.passage=s.p==null?null:d.pass[s.p];});});})(window.MAP_DATA["reading_6"]);
